// Auto-generated by build_questions.py from question-bank.md + boss-battles.md
const BANK = [{"id": "U1-Q1", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a parameter, because it describes part of the population", "a statistic, because it was computed from a sample", "a parameter, because it estimates the true defect rate", "a statistic, because the sample was selected randomly"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A quality manager at a factory that produces 12,000 light bulbs per day tests a random sample of 150 bulbs and finds that 2% are defective. The value 2% is best described as:</p>", "expl": "A statistic is any number computed from a sample; a parameter describes the whole population. (D states the right label for the wrong reason — randomness isn't what makes it a statistic.)"}, {"id": "U1-Q2", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["simple random sampling", "stratified sampling", "cluster sampling", "systematic sampling"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A pollster stands at a supermarket exit and interviews every 25th shopper who leaves. Which sampling method is this?</p>", "expl": "Selecting every kth individual is systematic sampling. It is NOT simple random sampling because not every possible <i>group</i> of shoppers has an equal chance of being the sample."}, {"id": "U1-Q3", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["stratified sampling", "cluster sampling", "systematic sampling", "simple random sampling"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A college wants student opinions on parking, so it randomly selects 20 students from <i>each</i> academic major. Which sampling method is this?</p>", "expl": "The population is divided into groups (strata = majors) and <i>some members of every group</i> are sampled. Cluster sampling would instead randomly pick a few majors and survey <i>everyone</i> in them — that's the classic mix-up."}, {"id": "U1-Q4", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a survey of farmers comparing yields of those who chose to use the fertilizer", "an observational study following a very large number of farms", "an experiment in which plots are randomly assigned to fertilizer or no fertilizer", "a study finding a strong, statistically significant correlation between fertilizer use and yield"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which study design allows a researcher to legitimately conclude that a new fertilizer <i>causes</i> increased crop yield?</p>", "expl": "Only random assignment balances lurking variables between groups, justifying cause-and-effect conclusions. [misconception: big-n observational study or significant correlation proves causation]"}, {"id": "U1-Q5", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["ice cream consumption impairs swimming ability", "drownings cause community stress, which increases ice cream sales", "a lurking variable, such as hot summer weather, drives both", "the data must have come from a biased sample"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Cities with higher ice cream sales also have more drownings. The most likely explanation is:</p>", "expl": "A confounding (lurking) variable — temperature — raises both. Association alone never identifies the causal direction."}, {"id": "U1-Q6", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["interval, because differences are meaningful but there is no true zero", "ratio, because temperature can be measured to any precision", "ordinal, because temperatures can be ranked from low to high", "nominal, because the degree values are just numeric labels"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Temperatures measured in degrees Fahrenheit have which level of measurement?</p>", "expl": "0°F doesn't mean \"no temperature,\" so ratios (80° is NOT \"twice as hot\" as 40°) are meaningless → interval, not ratio."}, {"id": "U1-Q7", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["qualitative", "quantitative continuous", "qualitative ordinal", "quantitative discrete"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>\"Number of siblings a student has\" is what type of variable?</p>", "expl": "It's a count — numeric (quantitative) and takes only separate whole-number values (discrete). Continuous variables are <i>measured</i> (time, weight) and can take any value in an interval."}, {"id": "U1-Q8", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the sample is too small relative to the size of the city", "voluntary response samples are biased no matter how large they are", "poll percentages always need a margin of error of at least 5%", "website polls cannot verify each respondent's identity"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A TV station's website poll gets 250,000 responses and finds 78% oppose a new tax. Why can't we trust the 78%?</p>", "expl": "People who self-select into polls differ systematically from the population (strong opinions overrepresented). Size doesn't fix bias — the 1936 Literary Digest poll had over 2 million responses and still called the election wrong. [misconception: bigger sample = representative sample]"}, {"id": "U1-Q9", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["random selection permits causal conclusions; random assignment permits generalization", "both exist primarily to reduce the sample size a study needs", "random assignment lets results generalize; random selection creates balanced groups", "random selection permits generalizing to the population; random assignment permits cause-and-effect conclusions"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Random <i>selection</i> (sampling) and random <i>assignment</i> serve different purposes. Which statement is correct?</p>", "expl": "Selection controls <i>who</i> the results describe (generalization); assignment controls <i>why</i> the groups differ (causation). A and C swap them — the most common error."}, {"id": "U1-Q10", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a convenience sample", "a simple random sample, since any student could have walked in", "a cluster sample, with the library as the cluster", "a systematic sample, taken in arrival order"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A researcher surveys the first 40 students who walk into the library. This is:</p>", "expl": "Taking whoever is easiest to reach is convenience sampling — prone to bias (library users differ from non-users). \"Could have walked in\" ≠ equal chance for every possible sample of 40."}, {"id": "U2-Q1", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["2.00", "5.00", "2.24", "4.00"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Find the sample standard deviation of the data set: 4, 8, 6, 5, 2.</p>", "expl": "Mean = 5; squared deviations sum to 20; s² = 20/(5−1) = 5; s = √5 ≈ 2.24. (A divides by n instead of n−1; B is the variance, not the SD; D is the population variance.)"}, {"id": "U2-Q2", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["mean &gt; median", "mean &lt; median", "mean = median", "mode &gt; mean"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>For a strongly right-skewed distribution (such as household incomes), which is most likely true?</p>", "expl": "The long right tail pulls the mean above the median. (For left-skewed data the mean is below the median; in symmetric data they're equal.)"}, {"id": "U2-Q3", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the history exam, because 90 is the higher score", "the history exam, because it is a full 10 points above the mean", "they were equally good, because both are above their class means", "the statistics exam, because its z-score (1.5) exceeds the history z-score (1.0)"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Maria scored 82 on a statistics exam (class mean 70, SD 8) and 90 on a history exam (class mean 80, SD 10). Relative to her classmates, which exam performance was better?</p>", "expl": "z = (82−70)/8 = 1.5 vs z = (90−80)/10 = 1.0. Relative standing is measured in standard deviations, not raw points."}, {"id": "U2-Q4", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["29", "32", "28", "−1"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A data set has Q1 = 10 and Q3 = 18. Using the 1.5×IQR rule, which value would be flagged as an outlier?</p>", "expl": "IQR = 8; fences are 10 − 12 = −2 and 18 + 12 = 30. Only 32 falls outside (−1 is inside, since −1 &gt; −2)."}, {"id": "U2-Q5", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["68%", "95%", "50%", "99.7%"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>IQ scores are bell-shaped with mean 100 and SD 15. By the Empirical Rule, about what percent of scores fall between 85 and 115?</p>", "expl": "85 to 115 is mean ± 1 SD → about 68%. (±2 SD ≈ 95%, ±3 SD ≈ 99.7%.)"}, {"id": "U2-Q6", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the entire range of the data", "the interval mean ± 1 standard deviation", "the middle 95% of the data", "from Q1 to Q3 — the middle 50% of the data"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>In a boxplot, the box itself spans:</p>", "expl": "The box runs Q1 to Q3 (width = IQR) with the median line inside; whiskers extend toward min/max (or to the fences)."}, {"id": "U2-Q7", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["Histogram II, because its bar heights vary more", "Histogram I, because its values sit farther from the center on average", "both the same, because they cover the same range", "impossible to determine without the raw data"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Two histograms use the same scale. Histogram I is flat (all bars equal height, values spread evenly from 0 to 100). Histogram II has one tall peak at 50 with short tails. Which data set has the larger standard deviation?</p>", "expl": "Variability is <i>horizontal</i> spread of values around the mean, not \"bumpiness\" of bar heights — a flat histogram has lots of data far from center; a tight peak has most data near the mean. [documented misconception: judging variability by bar height variation]"}, {"id": "U2-Q8", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the median, but not the mean", "both the mean and median equally", "the mean, but not (much) the median", "neither, because n = 20 is large enough to absorb it"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A data set of 20 home prices includes one mansion worth 10× the others. Adding the mansion mostly affects:</p>", "expl": "The mean uses every value's magnitude, so one extreme value drags it; the median only cares about order, so it barely moves. That's why we report medians for skewed data like incomes."}, {"id": "U2-Q9", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the mean", "the median", "the third quartile", "the minimum"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which of these is NOT part of the five-number summary?</p>", "expl": "Five-number summary = min, Q1, median, Q3, max. The mean isn't included (it's not resistant, and the summary is built from positions)."}, {"id": "U2-Q10", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["he answered 80% of the questions correctly", "he scored 80% of the maximum possible score", "80% of test-takers scored above him", "he scored at or above about 80% of test-takers"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Dev's exam score is at the 80th percentile. This means:</p>", "expl": "A percentile is a position in the distribution, not a percent-correct. (C reverses it.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q1", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.90", "0.20", "0.10", "0.70"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, and P(A and B) = 0.2. Find P(A or B).</p>", "expl": "P(A or B) = 0.5 + 0.4 − 0.2 = 0.7. (A forgets to subtract the overlap — which double-counts outcomes in both events.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q2", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["yes, because they have no outcomes in common", "no — if A occurs, B cannot occur, so knowing A changes the probability of B", "yes, because P(A) + P(B) is less than 1", "cannot be determined without knowing P(A and B)"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Events A and B are mutually exclusive, with P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.2. Are A and B independent?</p>", "expl": "Mutually exclusive events (with nonzero probabilities) are <i>maximally dependent</i>: P(B|A) = 0 ≠ P(B) = 0.2. (D is wrong because mutually exclusive <i>tells</i> you P(A and B) = 0.) [documented misconception: disjoint = independent]"}, {"id": "U3-Q3", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.70", "0.54", "0.35", "0.65"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>100 students: 30 men passed, 20 men failed, 35 women passed, 15 women failed. What is P(passed | woman)?</p>", "expl": "Restrict to the 50 women: 35/50 = 0.70. (B is P(woman | passed) = 35/65 — the inverted conditional; C is the joint probability 35/100; D is the overall pass rate 65/100.) [documented misconception: confusing P(A|B) with P(B|A)]"}, {"id": "U3-Q4", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["mutually exclusive", "dependent, since P(A and B) is not zero", "independent, since P(A) × P(B) = P(A and B)", "complementary"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5, and P(A and B) = 0.15. The events A and B are:</p>", "expl": "0.3 × 0.5 = 0.15 = P(A and B), which is exactly the definition of independence. (B has it backwards — disjointness, not dependence, is about zero overlap.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q5", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["less than 1/2, because tails is now \"due\"", "exactly 1/2", "more than 1/2, because the coin is on a streak", "1/64, the probability of six heads in a row"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A fair coin lands heads 5 times in a row. The probability of heads on the next flip is:</p>", "expl": "Independent trials have no memory. (D confuses the probability of the whole sequence-in-advance with the next single flip.) [documented misconception: gambler's fallacy]"}, {"id": "U3-Q6", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.0059", "0.0769", "0.0023", "0.0045"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Two cards are drawn from a standard deck <i>without replacement</i>. The probability both are aces is:</p>", "expl": "(4/52)(3/51) = 1/221 ≈ 0.0045. (A is (4/52)² — with replacement, ignoring that the first ace is gone; B is just 4/52.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q7", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["720", "30", "120", "1,000"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A club of 10 members must select a 3-person committee (order doesn't matter). How many different committees are possible?</p>", "expl": "10C3 = 10!/(3!·7!) = 120. (A is 10P3 = 720 — permutations, which wrongly counts the same 3 people in different orders as different committees; D is 10³.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q8", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a sum of 7 — it can occur six ways (1+6, 2+5, 3+4, 4+3, 5+2, 6+1), while 12 occurs only one way", "both are equally likely, since every sum from 2 to 12 has the same chance", "a sum of 12 — matching dice are just as common as any other specific pair", "it cannot be determined without rolling the dice many times"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Rolling two fair dice, which is more likely: a sum of 7 or a sum of 12?</p>", "expl": "Sums are NOT equally likely; you must count the underlying outcomes: P(7) = 6/36 vs P(12) = 1/36. [documented misconception: equiprobability bias]"}, {"id": "U3-Q9", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["yes — rain was predicted and did not occur", "not necessarily — 80% means that on days with conditions like this, it rains about 8 times in 10", "yes — any probability above 50% counts as a prediction of rain", "no — probability forecasts can never be evaluated"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A forecaster said there was an 80% chance of rain, and it did not rain. Was the forecast wrong?</p>", "expl": "A probability is a long-run relative frequency, not a yes/no prediction about one day. (Forecasts <i>can</i> be evaluated — over many 80% days, it should rain about 80% of the time — so D overcorrects.) [documented misconception: outcome orientation]"}, {"id": "U3-Q10", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the counts of heads and tails become exactly equal", "after a run of heads, tails becomes more likely until balance is restored", "the proportion of heads tends to get closer and closer to 0.5", "the difference between the number of heads and tails shrinks toward zero"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>As a fair coin is flipped more and more times, which statement is true?</p>", "expl": "The Law of Large Numbers is about the <i>proportion</i> converging — the raw count difference can actually grow. (B is the gambler's fallacy again.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q1", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["P(X): 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.3", "P(X): 0.2, 0.3, 0.6, −0.1", "P(X): 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5", "P(X): 0.25, 0.25, 0.30, 0.10"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which of the following is a valid probability distribution for X = 0, 1, 2, 3?</p>", "expl": "Requirements: every probability in [0, 1] AND the sum equals exactly 1. (B has a negative; C sums to 1.1; D sums to 0.9.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q2", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["+$2.00", "−$5.00", "−$3.00", "$0.00, since someone has to win"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A charity sells 1,000 raffle tickets at $5 each; one winner receives $2,000. The expected value of buying one ticket (net of its cost) is:</p>", "expl": "E = (2000)(1/1000) − 5 = 2 − 5 = −$3. (A forgets you paid for the ticket; B forgets you might win.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q3", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.5", "1.9", "2.0", "0.25"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>X takes values 0, 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 0.3. The mean of X is:</p>", "expl": "μ = Σx·P(x) = 0(0.1) + 1(0.2) + 2(0.4) + 3(0.3) = 1.9. (A averages the x values 0–3 without weighting by their probabilities; C is just the most likely value.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q4", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["drawing 5 cards from a deck without replacement and counting hearts", "rolling a die repeatedly until the first six appears", "recording the exact weight of each of 10 packages", "asking 20 randomly selected voters whether they support a measure, and counting yes responses"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Which scenario can be modeled by a binomial distribution?</p>", "expl": "Fixed n, two outcomes, (essentially) independent trials, constant p. (A fails independence/constant p — no replacement; B has no fixed n — that's geometric; C isn't counting successes at all.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q5", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.0052", "0.3828", "0.2335", "0.0900"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For a binomial variable with n = 10 and p = 0.3, find P(X = 2).</p>", "expl": "P(2) = 10C2 (0.3)²(0.7)⁸ = 45(0.09)(0.0576) ≈ 0.2335. (A forgets the 10C2 counting factor; B is the <i>cumulative</i> P(X ≤ 2); D is just (0.3)².) TI-84: <code>binompdf(10,.3,2)</code>."}, {"id": "U4-Q6", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.672", "0.328", "1.000, since 5 × 0.2 = 1", "0.200"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Five independent trials each succeed with probability 0.2. P(at least one success) is:</p>", "expl": "P(at least one) = 1 − P(none) = 1 − (0.8)⁵ = 1 − 0.328 = 0.672. (B stops at P(none); C illegally adds probabilities across trials.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q7", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["3.00", "2.10", "1.73", "1.45"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>For a binomial variable with n = 10, p = 0.3, the standard deviation of X is:</p>", "expl": "σ = √(npq) = √(10 × 0.3 × 0.7) = √2.1 ≈ 1.449. (A is the mean np; B is the variance npq — forgot the square root.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q8", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["no — 62 is within two standard deviations of 50", "yes — 62 exceeds 50 + 2(5) = 60", "no — with 100 flips, any count between 35 and 65 is typical", "yes — any count above 55 is unusual for a fair coin"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A fair coin is flipped 100 times. Using the μ ± 2σ \"unusual values\" rule, would 62 heads be unusual?</p>", "expl": "μ = np = 50, σ = √(100·0.5·0.5) = 5, so the usual range is 40 to 60; 62 falls outside."}, {"id": "U4-Q9", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["every player loses exactly 50 cents on each play", "it is impossible to come out ahead in any single play", "over many plays, players lose an average of about 50 cents per play", "after exactly 10 plays, a player will be down exactly $5"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A casino game has expected value −$0.50 per play. The correct interpretation is:</p>", "expl": "Expected value is a <i>long-run average</i>, not a guarantee about any single play or short run. [documented misconception: outcome orientation applied to E(X)]"}, {"id": "U4-Q10", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the exact amount of time a customer waits on hold", "the number of calls a call center receives in an hour", "the number of defective items in a batch of 50", "the number showing when a fair die is rolled"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which of the following is a <i>continuous</i> random variable?</p>", "expl": "Time is measured, not counted — it can take any value in an interval. B, C, D are all counts (discrete)."}, {"id": "U5-Q1", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.6554", "0.3446", "0.4000", "0.0446"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Adult women's heights are N(63, 5) inches. P(a randomly chosen woman is taller than 65 inches) = ?</p>", "expl": "z = (65−63)/5 = 0.40 → area to the RIGHT = 1 − 0.6554 = 0.3446. (A is the area to the left — forgot the complement; C is the z-value misread as probability.) TI-84: <code>normalcdf(65,1E99,63,5)</code>."}, {"id": "U5-Q2", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["56.59 inches", "71.23 inches", "64.50 inches", "69.41 inches"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Heights are N(63, 5). What height separates the tallest 10% of women (the 90th percentile)?</p>", "expl": "z₀.₉₀ ≈ 1.28 → x = 63 + 1.28(5) ≈ 69.4. (A used z = −1.28, giving the 10th percentile; B used z = 1.645, the 95th.) TI-84: <code>invNorm(0.90,63,5)</code>."}, {"id": "U5-Q3", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the distribution of the individual data values in the sample", "the population distribution, once n ≥ 30", "the distribution of the sample mean across all possible samples", "every distribution involved in the problem"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The Central Limit Theorem says that for large n, which distribution is approximately normal regardless of the population's shape?</p>", "expl": "Only the <i>sampling distribution of x̄</i> (and sums) becomes normal. A large sample from a skewed population is still skewed — it mirrors the population. [documented misconception: \"the data become normal\"]"}, {"id": "U5-Q4", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["2.5", "15, the same as the population", "0.42", "6.0"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A population has standard deviation 15. For random samples of size n = 36, the standard deviation of the sample mean (the standard error) is:</p>", "expl": "SE = σ/√n = 15/6 = 2.5. (B is the documented SD-vs-SE confusion — averages vary <i>less</i> than individuals; C divides by n instead of √n.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q5", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.4207", "0.8849", "0.0228", "0.1151"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>IQ scores: μ = 100, σ = 15. For a random sample of n = 36 people, find P(x̄ &gt; 103).</p>", "expl": "SE = 15/√36 = 2.5; z = (103−100)/2.5 = 1.2 → P = 0.1151. (A uses σ = 15 instead of the SE — the classic error, giving z = 0.2; B is the left-tail area.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q6", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["looks more and more like the population distribution", "becomes narrower — its standard error is cut in half", "keeps the same spread but becomes more symmetric", "becomes wider, since larger samples can include more extreme values"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>As sample size increases from 25 to 100, the sampling distribution of x̄:</p>", "expl": "SE = σ/√n: quadrupling n halves the SE (√25=5 → √100=10). It's the <i>sample histogram</i> that comes to resemble the population — not the sampling distribution. [documented misconception #3]"}, {"id": "U5-Q7", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the sample's histogram resembles the population for large n, while the sampling distribution of x̄ is narrower than both", "the sample's histogram becomes normal for large n, whatever the population looks like", "the sampling distribution of x̄ has the same spread as the population", "population distribution, sample distribution, and sampling distribution are three names for the same idea"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes the three distributions in a sampling problem?</p>", "expl": "Three different objects: the population (fixed), one sample's data (resembles the population as n grows), and the distribution of x̄ over all samples (narrower: σ/√n, and approximately normal by the CLT)."}, {"id": "U5-Q8", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the large hospital — more births means more chances for such days", "both about the same — the probability of a boy is the same everywhere", "the small hospital — small samples fluctuate more around 50%", "neither — days with over 60% boys essentially never happen"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A small hospital averages 15 births/day; a large hospital averages 60. Over a year, which hospital records more days on which over 60% of births are boys?</p>", "expl": "Smaller samples have larger standard errors, so extreme proportions occur more often. Most people answer B. [documented misconception: insensitivity to sample size — Tversky &amp; Kahneman's hospital problem]"}, {"id": "U5-Q9", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.9332", "0.4332", "0.1500", "0.0668"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>P(Z &lt; −1.50) = ?</p>", "expl": "By symmetry, P(Z &lt; −1.5) = P(Z &gt; 1.5) = 1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668. (A is P(Z &lt; +1.5); B is the mean-to-z table area; C just echoes the 1.5.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q10", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["85", "70", "130", "97"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Scores are N(100, 15). What score is exactly 2 standard deviations <i>below</i> the mean?</p>", "expl": "x = μ + zσ = 100 + (−2)(15) = 70. (A is only 1 SD below; C is 2 SD <i>above</i>; D subtracts 2 points + 1... a careless-reading trap.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q1", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["95% of commuters take between 21.5 and 26.3 minutes", "there is a 95% probability that μ lies between 21.5 and 26.3", "we are 95% confident this interval captures μ — the method produces intervals that capture the true mean in about 95% of all samples", "95% of future sample means will fall between 21.5 and 26.3"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A 95% confidence interval for the mean commute time is (21.5, 26.3) minutes. The correct interpretation is:</p>", "expl": "The 95% describes the long-run success rate of the <i>procedure</i>. A is the \"95% of the data\" misreading; B treats the fixed μ as random; D is also false (it's about the parameter, not future x̄'s). [the most-documented CI misconceptions]"}, {"id": "U6-Q2", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the t distribution, because σ is unknown", "the z distribution, because n ≥ 30", "the z distribution, because s is known from the data", "either one — they give identical intervals"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>To build a confidence interval for a population mean using the sample standard deviation s with n = 64, you should use:</p>", "expl": "The rule is about whether σ (the <i>population</i> SD) is known — it almost never is, so use t with df = 63. With large n, t and z are <i>close</i> but not identical, and \"n ≥ 30 → z\" is the documented shortcut-turned-misconception. [misconception: t is only for small samples]"}, {"id": "U6-Q3", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["(76.08, 83.92)", "(75.87, 84.13)", "(79.17, 80.83)", "(59.36, 100.64)"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>n = 25, x̄ = 80, s = 10. The 95% confidence interval for μ is (t* = 2.064):</p>", "expl": "SE = 10/√25 = 2; ME = 2.064 × 2 = 4.13 → 80 ± 4.13. (A used z* = 1.96 instead of t*; C used s/n = 0.4 for the SE; D used s itself without dividing by √n.) TI-84: <code>TInterval</code>."}, {"id": "U6-Q4", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["(0.560, 0.640)", "(0.519, 0.681)", "(0.480, 0.720)", "(0.504, 0.696)"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A sample of 100 voters finds 60 support a measure. The 95% confidence interval for the population proportion is:</p>", "expl": "SE = √(0.6×0.4/100) = 0.049; ME = 1.96 × 0.049 = 0.096 → 0.6 ± 0.096. (B used z* = 1.645, the 90% value.) TI-84: <code>1-PropZInt</code>."}, {"id": "U6-Q5", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1,068", "1,067", "752", "34"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>You want to estimate a population proportion to within E = 0.03 with 95% confidence and have no prior estimate. The required sample size is:</p>", "expl": "n = (1.96)²(0.25)/(0.03)² = 1067.1 → ALWAYS round UP → 1068. (B rounds down — the classic point-loser; C used z* = 1.645.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q6", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["increasing the confidence level from 95% to 99%", "decreasing the sample size", "increasing the sample size", "requiring the interval to capture μ with higher probability"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which change makes a confidence interval NARROWER?</p>", "expl": "Larger n shrinks the SE (σ/√n). Higher confidence (A, D) requires a <i>wider</i> net to be more sure of catching μ. [documented misconception: students invert both effects]"}, {"id": "U6-Q7", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["x̄ = 12, ME = 8", "x̄ = 20, ME = 8", "x̄ = 16, ME = 8", "x̄ = 16, ME = 4"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A confidence interval for μ is (12, 20). The point estimate and margin of error are:</p>", "expl": "The interval is centered at x̄ = (12+20)/2 = 16, and ME is the <i>half</i>-width: (20−12)/2 = 4. (C takes the full width as the ME.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q8", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["μ has a 95% chance of being between 4.1 and 7.9", "this fixed interval either contains μ or it doesn't — the 95% describes the method, not this one interval", "if we sampled again, there is a 95% chance of getting this same interval", "95% of the sample's data values lie between 4.1 and 7.9"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>After computing a specific 95% CI of (4.1, 7.9), which statement is true?</p>", "expl": "Once computed, nothing is random anymore: μ is a fixed (unknown) number and the interval is fixed. The probability statement applies to the procedure across repeated samples."}, {"id": "U6-Q9", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the sample mean x̄, because the interval is built symmetrically around it", "the population mean μ", "the majority of the individual data values", "the sample median"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which value is <i>guaranteed</i> to lie inside a confidence interval for μ?</p>", "expl": "The interval is x̄ ± ME — x̄ is always the center. μ is what we're <i>hoping</i> to capture (and miss 5% of the time); the data values themselves can mostly lie outside."}, {"id": "U6-Q10", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["96", "68", "97", "10"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>To estimate a population mean to within E = 2 units with 95% confidence, with σ = 10, the required sample size is:</p>", "expl": "n = (1.96 × 10/2)² = 96.04 → round UP → 97. (A rounds down; B used 1.645.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q1", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["there is a 3% chance that H₀ is true", "there is a 97% chance this result would replicate", "the result happened by chance 3% of the time", "if μ really were 50, results at least this extreme would occur in about 3% of samples"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A test of H₀: μ = 50 gives a p-value of 0.03. This means:</p>", "expl": "The p-value is P(data this extreme | H₀ true) — a statement about <i>data</i> assuming H₀, never about the probability of H₀ itself. A is the single most common misconception in all of intro stats; B is the documented \"replication fallacy.\""}, {"id": "U7-Q2", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["H₀: x̄ = 50 vs Hₐ: x̄ ≠ 50", "H₀: μ = 50 vs Hₐ: μ ≠ 50", "H₀: μ ≠ 50 vs Hₐ: μ = 50", "H₀: x̄ = 50 vs Hₐ: μ ≠ 50"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Which is a properly stated pair of hypotheses?</p>", "expl": "Hypotheses are claims about population <i>parameters</i> (μ, p) — never sample statistics (we <i>know</i> x̄, no need to test it). And H₀ always carries the equality. [documented misconception: hypotheses about the sample]"}, {"id": "U7-Q3", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["accept H₀ — we have shown the null hypothesis is true", "the data prove there is no effect", "fail to reject H₀ — the data don't give sufficient evidence against it, which is not proof that it's true", "reject Hₐ and conclude H₀ is correct"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A test gives p-value = 0.42 with α = 0.05. The correct conclusion is:</p>", "expl": "\"Fail to reject\" ≠ \"accept\": the study may simply lack power. Absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. [documented misconception #6]"}, {"id": "U7-Q4", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["concluding the drug works when it actually does not", "concluding the drug doesn't work when it actually does", "failing to reach any conclusion from the data", "choosing a significance level that is too small"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>H₀ says a new drug has no effect. A Type I error here means:</p>", "expl": "Type I = rejecting a TRUE H₀ (false positive); its probability is α. (B describes Type II, the false negative — students swap these constantly.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q5", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["reject H₀, since 56% is clearly more than 50%", "fail to reject H₀, since the p-value 0.09 exceeds α = 0.05", "reject H₀, since the p-value is less than 0.10", "accept H₀ and conclude exactly half prefer Brand X"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Claim: exactly half of customers prefer Brand X. From n = 200, p̂ = 0.56, the two-tailed test gives z ≈ 1.70 and p-value ≈ 0.09. At α = 0.05, you should:</p>", "expl": "Decision rule: reject only when p-value ≤ α. 0.09 &gt; 0.05 → not enough evidence. (A judges by the raw difference instead of the test; C uses the wrong threshold; D \"accepts.\") TI-84: <code>1-PropZTest</code>."}, {"id": "U7-Q6", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["5% of the data will be treated as outliers and discarded", "the probability that H₀ is true is 5%", "we will be wrong in 5% of all decisions we ever make", "if H₀ is true, we are accepting a 5% risk of wrongly rejecting it"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Setting α = 0.05 means:</p>", "expl": "α is the Type I error rate — a <i>conditional</i> probability (given H₀ true), chosen before the test. (B inverts the conditional; C drops the condition.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q7", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["statistically significant, but probably not practically important", "both statistically and practically significant, since p is so small", "not statistically significant, because 0.2 lb is tiny", "unreliable — samples that large distort p-values"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A study with n = 2,000,000 finds a weight-loss pill produces an average loss of 0.2 lb, p &lt; 0.001. The best characterization:</p>", "expl": "Huge samples detect even trivial effects. Significance measures <i>evidence of existence</i>, not <i>size or importance</i> of the effect. [documented misconception #7]"}, {"id": "U7-Q8", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["Hₐ: p ≠ 0.30", "Hₐ: p &lt; 0.30", "Hₐ: p &gt; 0.30", "Hₐ: p ≥ 0.30"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>You want to test the claim \"more than 30% of students work full time.\" The alternative hypothesis is:</p>", "expl": "\"More than\" → right-tailed alternative; the claim being tested for support goes in Hₐ (strict inequality). (D can't be an alternative — it includes equality, which belongs to H₀.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q9", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["t = 0.33", "t = 12.0", "t = 0.06", "t = 2.0"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Test H₀: μ = 50 with x̄ = 52, s = 6, n = 36. The test statistic is:</p>", "expl": "t = (52 − 50)/(6/√36) = 2/1 = 2.0 with df = 35. (A divides by s instead of the SE; B divides by s/n.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q10", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["rejecting a true null hypothesis", "failing to reject a false null hypothesis", "the null hypothesis being false", "making an error of either type"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>β is the probability of:</p>", "expl": "β = P(Type II error); power = 1 − β is the chance of correctly detecting a real effect. (A is α/Type I.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q1", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a paired t-test on the before/after differences", "a two-sample t-test treating the groups as independent", "a one-proportion z-test on the fraction who improved", "a chi-square test of independence"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>To evaluate a training program, each employee's productivity is measured before and after training. The appropriate procedure is:</p>", "expl": "Same subjects measured twice → the samples are dependent (paired); analyze the differences d = after − before. (B wrongly ignores the pairing, wasting power.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q2", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["30", "55", "15", "110"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>In a chi-square test of independence, a cell's row total is 60, its column total is 50, and the grand total is 200. The expected count for that cell is:</p>", "expl": "E = (row total)(column total)/(grand total) = 60 × 50/200 = 15."}, {"id": "U8-Q3", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the two variables are related in the population", "the observed counts equal the expected counts exactly", "all the population proportions are equal to each other", "the two variables are independent in the population"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>In a chi-square test of independence, the null hypothesis states:</p>", "expl": "H₀ = independence (no association); rejecting it gives evidence of an association. (Chi-square null hypotheses are always the \"no relationship\" claim.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q4", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a two-sample t-test", "a two-proportion z-test", "a paired t-test", "a one-way ANOVA"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>You want to compare the proportion of men versus women who voted yes on a measure. The appropriate procedure is:</p>", "expl": "Comparing two <i>proportions</i> from independent groups → two-proportion z. (A is for two <i>means</i>.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q5", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["12", "11", "6", "7"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A contingency table has 3 rows and 4 columns. The degrees of freedom for the test of independence are:</p>", "expl": "df = (r−1)(c−1) = 2 × 3 = 6. (A is r×c; B is rc−1, the GOF-style formula misapplied.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q6", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the observed counts are far from what independence would predict — evidence against H₀", "the observed counts closely match the expected counts", "the sample size must have been too small", "the two variables must be causally related"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A large chi-square test statistic means:</p>", "expl": "χ² = Σ(O−E)²/E grows as observed counts deviate from expected; large χ² → small p-value → reject independence. (D overreaches: association ≠ causation, even when significant.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q7", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["group 1's mean is significantly larger", "no significant difference — zero is a plausible value for the difference", "group 2's mean is significantly larger", "the samples must have been paired"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A 95% confidence interval for μ₁ − μ₂ is (−1.2, 3.4). At α = 0.05 (two-tailed), you should conclude:</p>", "expl": "The interval contains 0, so \"no difference\" can't be ruled out — equivalent to failing to reject H₀: μ₁ = μ₂."}, {"id": "U8-Q8", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["compare the variances of two populations", "test whether a single mean equals a claimed value", "compare two proportions across several groups", "test whether three or more population means are all equal"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>One-way ANOVA is used to:</p>", "expl": "ANOVA generalizes the two-sample t to 3+ group means, using the F statistic (between-group variation / within-group variation). (A confuses it with an F test for variances.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q1", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a weak negative linear relationship", "a strong negative linear relationship", "a strong relationship proving TV watching lowers scores", "a computational error, since r cannot be negative"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A correlation of r = −0.85 between hours of TV and exam score indicates:</p>", "expl": "|r| near 1 = strong; the sign gives direction. (C smuggles in causation — correlation can't deliver it.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q2", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["eating breakfast causes higher GPAs, since p &lt; 0.01", "breakfast has no real effect — observational studies prove nothing", "the relationship is almost certainly due to chance", "breakfast and GPA are associated, but confounding variables could explain the link"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>An observational study finds students who eat breakfast have significantly higher GPAs (p &lt; 0.01). The justified conclusion is:</p>", "expl": "Significance rules out chance as a likely explanation, but in an observational study it cannot rule out confounders (sleep habits, family structure, etc.). [documented misconception: significant association → causation]"}, {"id": "U9-Q3", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.6", "0.4", "0.8", "3.2"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>For a data set, r = 0.8, the SD of y is 4, and the SD of x is 2. The slope of the least-squares line is:</p>", "expl": "b = r(s_y/s_x) = 0.8 × (4/2) = 1.6. (B inverts the ratio; C forgets the ratio entirely; D multiplies by s_y alone.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q4", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["13.6", "95.2", "23.2", "19.2"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The regression line is ŷ = 12 + 1.6x. The predicted y when x = 7 is:</p>", "expl": "ŷ = 12 + 1.6(7) = 12 + 11.2 = 23.2. (A adds the slope once instead of multiplying; B multiplies the whole expression by 7.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q5", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["each extra hour of study causes a 2.5-point score increase", "every student who studies one more hour scores exactly 2.5 points higher", "2.5% of the variation in scores is explained by study time", "for each additional hour studied, the predicted score increases by about 2.5 points on average"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>ŷ = 50 + 2.5x predicts exam score from hours studied. The best interpretation of 2.5 is:</p>", "expl": "Slope language must be <i>predicted/average</i> change (\"on average\"), not deterministic (B) or causal (A — this would need a randomized experiment). [documented misconception: deterministic/causal slope interpretation]"}, {"id": "U9-Q6", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["60%", "36%", "77%", "40%"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>The correlation between x and y is r = 0.6. What percent of the variation in y is explained by the linear relationship with x?</p>", "expl": "r² = 0.36 → 36%. (A reads r itself as the percent — the #1 error; C treats 0.6 as if it were r², taking √0.6.) [documented misconception: r vs r²]"}, {"id": "U9-Q7", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["fine, because the line fit the data well", "fine, as long as r is close to 1", "unreliable extrapolation — the linear pattern may not continue beyond the observed ages", "acceptable, provided the prediction is reported as an average"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A regression of weight on age was fit using children aged 2–10. Using the line to predict the weight of a 40-year-old is:</p>", "expl": "The fitted relationship is only supported inside the observed x-range (2–10); growth obviously doesn't continue linearly to age 40. [documented misconception: extrapolation]"}, {"id": "U9-Q8", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["there is no LINEAR relationship — though a strong curved relationship is still possible", "there is no relationship of any kind between the variables", "the two variables must be independent", "the regression line cannot be computed"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>For a data set, r ≈ 0. The correct conclusion is:</p>", "expl": "r measures only <i>linear</i> association: a perfect U-shaped (parabolic) relationship can give r = 0. (B and C overstate; D is false — the line exists, it's just flat and useless.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q9", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the minimum possible exam score is 50", "studying zero hours causes a score of exactly 50", "the line crosses the x-axis at 50", "the predicted score for someone who studies 0 hours is 50 — meaningful only if x = 0 is within the data's range"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>In ŷ = 50 + 2.5x (score from hours studied), the intercept 50 means:</p>", "expl": "The intercept is the prediction at x = 0, and interpreting it requires x = 0 to make sense and be in the data's scope. [documented misconception: over-interpreting the intercept]"}, {"id": "U9-Q10", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["−4", "4", "56", "1.15"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A data point has y = 30, and the regression line predicts ŷ = 26. The residual for that point is:</p>", "expl": "Residual = observed − predicted = 30 − 26 = +4; positive residual = point lies <i>above</i> the line. (A reverses the subtraction — the most common sign error.)"}, {"id": "MX-Q1", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a one-proportion z-interval", "a z-interval for the mean with σ known", "a t-interval for the mean", "a chi-square test"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Estimate the average commute time of all students at a college from a random sample of 45 students (population SD unknown). Use:</p>", "expl": "Goal = estimate a MEAN, σ unknown → t-interval, df = 44."}, {"id": "MX-Q2", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a one-proportion z-test", "a one-sample t-test", "a paired t-test", "a two-proportion z-test"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Test whether a coin is fair using the results of 500 flips. Use:</p>", "expl": "The data are yes/no (heads or not) and the claim is about a single proportion (H₀: p = 0.5)."}, {"id": "MX-Q3", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["one-way ANOVA", "linear regression", "a two-sample t-test", "a chi-square test of independence"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Is college major (5 categories) related to preferred study location (3 categories)? Use:</p>", "expl": "Two <i>categorical</i> variables, one sample, question of association → chi-square independence with df = (5−1)(3−1) = 8."}, {"id": "MX-Q4", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "linear regression", "a one-proportion z-interval", "a paired t-test"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Predict starting salary from years of work experience. Use:</p>", "expl": "Two <i>quantitative</i> variables with one predicting the other → least-squares regression."}, {"id": "MX-Q5", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["three separate two-sample t-tests at α = 0.05 each", "a chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "a two-proportion z-test", "one-way ANOVA"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Compare mean recovery times under three different physical-therapy programs. Use:</p>", "expl": "Three or more means → ANOVA. (A inflates the overall Type I error rate — that's exactly the problem ANOVA exists to avoid.)"}, {"id": "MX-Q6", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["strong evidence that the drug causes improvement — random assignment justifies the causal claim", "association only — correlation never implies causation", "the drug works for 99.9% of patients", "H₀ has been proven false"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A randomized, double-blind experiment finds the treatment group improved significantly more than placebo (p = 0.001). You may conclude:</p>", "expl": "\"Correlation isn't causation\" applies to <i>observational</i> data; a randomized experiment is precisely the design that licenses causal conclusions. (This question catches students who over-apply the slogan.)"}, {"id": "MX-Q7", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["μ", "σ", "x̄", "p (the population proportion)"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which symbol represents a statistic (not a parameter)?</p>", "expl": "Sample quantities: x̄, s, p̂. Population quantities: μ, σ, p."}, {"id": "MX-Q8", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["large samples make the population itself normal", "by the CLT, the sampling distribution of x̄ is approximately normal for large n", "skewness disappears from the data once n &gt; 30", "it isn't valid — t procedures require a normal population at every sample size"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A population of incomes is strongly right-skewed. Why is it still valid to use normal-based inference for x̄ with n = 100?</p>", "expl": "The CLT is about the distribution of x̄, which normalizes as n grows even though the population and the sample stay skewed."}, {"id": "MX-Q9", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["reject H₀, because 20 lies outside the interval", "fail to reject H₀, because 20 is close to 21.5", "accept H₀, because the interval is narrow", "conclude nothing — CIs cannot be used to test hypotheses"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A 95% CI for μ is (21.5, 26.3). Testing H₀: μ = 20 against Hₐ: μ ≠ 20 at α = 0.05, you should:</p>", "expl": "A 95% CI contains exactly the values that would NOT be rejected at α = 0.05 (two-tailed). 20 is outside → reject."}, {"id": "MX-Q10", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a very large convenience sample", "random assignment of subjects to treatment groups", "a high response rate among volunteers", "a random sample drawn from that population"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Which gives the strongest basis for GENERALIZING results to an entire population?</p>", "expl": "Generalization comes from random <i>selection</i>. Random <i>assignment</i> (B) buys causal validity, not representativeness — the two pillars are independent of each other."}, {"id": "U1-Q11", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["simple random sampling", "cluster sampling", "voluntary response sampling", "systematic sampling"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A podcast host asks listeners to text 1 for \"yes\" or 2 for \"no,\" and 5,000 texts arrive. The sampling method is:</p>", "expl": "Listeners self-selected — the texts came from whoever cared enough to respond, which biases the result regardless of the 5,000 count."}, {"id": "U1-Q12", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["plant height", "fertilizer type", "the garden plots", "the number of plants"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>In the study \"plants given Fertilizer X grow taller than unfertilized plants,\" the RESPONSE variable is:</p>", "expl": "The response is the measured outcome (height); fertilizer type is the explanatory variable."}, {"id": "U1-Q13", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["nominal", "ratio", "interval", "ordinal"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>\"Class rank (1st, 2nd, 3rd, …)\" has which level of measurement?</p>", "expl": "Ranks order the students, but the gap between 1st and 2nd needn't equal the gap between 2nd and 3rd — order without meaningful differences = ordinal."}, {"id": "U1-Q14", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["a random sample of at least 10% of the population", "data collected from EVERY member of the population", "any survey run by a government agency", "a study that uses stratified sampling"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A census is:</p>", "expl": "Census = the whole population; everything smaller is a sample."}, {"id": "U1-Q15", "unit": 1, "kind": "bank", "options": ["nonresponse bias", "undercoverage", "too small a sample", "leading question wording, which produces response bias"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A survey asks: \"Don't you agree that the cafeteria's food is overpriced?\" The biggest flaw is:</p>", "expl": "The phrasing pushes respondents toward \"yes\" — answers reflect the wording, not true opinion."}, {"id": "U2-Q11", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["mean 8, median 9", "mean 8, median 7", "mean 7, median 8", "mean 9, median 7"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>For the data 5, 7, 7, 9, 12 the mean and median are:</p>", "expl": "Mean = 40/5 = 8; the middle of the sorted list is 7."}, {"id": "U2-Q12", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["2", "8", "4", "16"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A data set has standard deviation s = 4. Its variance is:</p>", "expl": "Variance = s² = 16. (A takes the square root again; B doubles.)"}, {"id": "U2-Q13", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the mean increases by 10; the standard deviation is unchanged", "both the mean and standard deviation increase by 10", "the mean is unchanged; the standard deviation increases by 10", "neither changes"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Ten points are added to every score in a data set. What happens?</p>", "expl": "Shifting every value moves the center but not the spread — distances between values are unchanged."}, {"id": "U2-Q14", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the average of the 4th and 5th values", "the 4th value", "the 5th value", "any value between the 4th and 6th"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For 9 ordered data values, the median is:</p>", "expl": "With odd n, the median is the single middle value: position (9+1)/2 = 5."}, {"id": "U2-Q15", "unit": 2, "kind": "bank", "options": ["2.5%", "5%", "16%", "0.3%"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Scores are bell-shaped with mean 40 and SD 5. About what percent exceed 50?</p>", "expl": "50 is 2 SDs above the mean; ~95% lie within ±2σ, leaving ~5% split between two tails → ~2.5% above."}, {"id": "U3-Q11", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.35", "0.50", "1.35", "0.65"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>P(A) = 0.35. P(A does not occur) = ?</p>", "expl": "Complement rule: 1 − 0.35 = 0.65."}, {"id": "U3-Q12", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.10", "0.30", "0.55", "0.11"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A and B are independent with P(A) = 0.6 and P(B) = 0.5. P(A and B) = ?</p>", "expl": "Independent → multiply: (0.6)(0.5) = 0.30. (A adds — that's the \"or\" rule's first step, not \"and.\")"}, {"id": "U3-Q13", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.12", "0.48", "0.30", "3.33"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>P(A) = 0.40 and P(A and B) = 0.12. P(B | A) = ?</p>", "expl": "P(B|A) = P(A and B)/P(A) = 0.12/0.40 = 0.30. (D divides the wrong way.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q14", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.99", "0.81", "0.90", "1.80"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Two independent smoke alarms each work with probability 0.9. P(at least one works) = ?</p>", "expl": "1 − P(both fail) = 1 − (0.1)(0.1) = 0.99. (B is P(both work); D illegally adds.)"}, {"id": "U3-Q15", "unit": 3, "kind": "bank", "options": ["20", "10", "25", "120"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>How many ways can 2 co-captains be chosen from 5 players (order doesn't matter)?</p>", "expl": "5C2 = 10. (A is 5P2 = 20, which wrongly counts AB and BA separately.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q11", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["5", "4", "15", "0.25"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>X ~ binomial with n = 20, p = 0.25. The mean of X is:</p>", "expl": "μ = np = 20(0.25) = 5."}, {"id": "U4-Q12", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["3.75", "5.00", "1.94", "2.24"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For that same X, the standard deviation is:</p>", "expl": "σ = √(npq) = √(20·0.25·0.75) = √3.75 ≈ 1.94. (A is the variance.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q13", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.0081", "0.2401", "0.7000", "0"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Four independent customers each buy with probability 0.3. P(NO ONE buys) = ?</p>", "expl": "P(none) = (0.7)⁴ = 0.2401. (A computes (0.3)⁴ — everyone buying.)"}, {"id": "U4-Q14", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["$10.00", "$0.10", "$5.00", "$1.00"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A game pays $10 with probability 0.1 and nothing otherwise. The fair (break-even) price to play is:</p>", "expl": "E(payout) = 10(0.1) = $1 — pay more than that and the house wins long-run."}, {"id": "U4-Q15", "unit": 4, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the number of heads in 20 coin flips", "the number of \"yes\" answers among 50 randomly polled voters", "the number of rolls of a die needed to get the first six", "the number of defective parts in a random batch of 10"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which is NOT a binomial random variable?</p>", "expl": "\"Trials until first success\" has no fixed n — that's geometric, not binomial."}, {"id": "U5-Q11", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.8997", "0.1003", "0.2810", "0.6400"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>P(Z &gt; 1.28) = ?</p>", "expl": "1 − 0.8997 = 0.1003 — about the top 10%. (A is the left-tail area.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q12", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.68", "0.95", "0.50", "0.997"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>IQ ~ N(100, 15). P(85 &lt; X &lt; 115) is approximately:</p>", "expl": "That's μ ± 1σ → ≈68% (exactly 0.6827)."}, {"id": "U5-Q13", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["56.7", "25.0", "47.5", "43.3"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Wait times ~ N(50, 10). The 25th percentile is about:</p>", "expl": "z₀.₂₅ ≈ −0.67 → 50 + (−0.67)(10) ≈ 43.3. (B confuses the percentile with the value.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q14", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.33", "12", "4", "3"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>σ = 12 and n = 9. The standard error of x̄ is:</p>", "expl": "12/√9 = 12/3 = 4. (A divides by n; D is √9.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q15", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["they can't be — the population must be normal", "skewness disappears from the sample data once n &gt; 30", "the population becomes normal as sampling continues", "the CLT makes the sampling distribution of x̄ approximately normal for large n"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A population is strongly skewed. Why can normal-based methods still be used for x̄ when n = 64?</p>", "expl": "Only the distribution of the sample MEAN normalizes; the population and the data stay skewed."}, {"id": "U6-Q11", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.96", "2.576", "1.645", "1.282"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The critical value z* for a 90% confidence interval is:</p>", "expl": "90% leaves 5% in each tail → z₀.₉₅ = 1.645. (A is 95%, B is 99%, D is 80%.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q12", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["(18.04, 21.96)", "(19.23, 20.77)", "(13.08, 26.92)", "(17.69, 22.31)"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>n = 9, x̄ = 20, s = 3, t* = 2.306. The 95% CI for μ is:</p>", "expl": "SE = 3/√9 = 1; ME = 2.306 → 20 ± 2.31. (A used z* = 1.96; C used s without √n.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q13", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["quadrupled", "doubled", "halved", "unchanged — change the confidence level instead"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>To cut a confidence interval's margin of error in HALF, the sample size must be:</p>", "expl": "ME ∝ 1/√n, so halving ME needs 4× the n."}, {"id": "U6-Q14", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["yes — n is at least 10", "no — np̂ = 2 successes, below the required 10", "yes — p̂ is below 0.5", "no — proportion intervals require n ≥ 100"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>n = 20 with p̂ = 0.10. Is the normal-based proportion CI valid?</p>", "expl": "Need at least ~10 successes AND 10 failures; 20(0.10) = 2 fails the check."}, {"id": "U6-Q15", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["narrower", "wider", "identical", "shifted to the left"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>From the same data, a 90% CI compared to a 95% CI is:</p>", "expl": "Less confidence demanded → smaller critical value → narrower interval."}, {"id": "U7-Q11", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["fail to reject H₀", "accept H₀", "raise α and retest", "reject H₀ — the result is significant even at the 0.01 level"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A test produces p-value = 0.003 with α = 0.01. The decision is:</p>", "expl": "0.003 ≤ 0.01 → reject."}, {"id": "U7-Q12", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["z = 0.06", "z = 6.0", "z = 1.22", "z = 2.45"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Test H₀: p = 0.40 with p̂ = 0.46, n = 100. The test statistic is about:</p>", "expl": "SE = √(0.40·0.60/100) = 0.049; z = 0.06/0.049 ≈ 1.22. (A forgets to divide by SE.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q13", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.11", "0.22", "0.89", "0.06"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>For z = 1.22, the TWO-tailed p-value is about:</p>", "expl": "2 × P(Z &gt; 1.22) = 2(0.1112) ≈ 0.22. (A forgets to double.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q14", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1 − β — the probability of detecting an effect that really exists", "α — the significance level", "β — the Type II error rate", "1 − α — the confidence level"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>The power of a hypothesis test is:</p>", "expl": "Power = P(reject H₀ | H₀ false). More power = fewer missed effects."}, {"id": "U7-Q15", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["raising α", "lowering α", "increasing the sample size", "switching to a one-tailed test"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The only change that reduces BOTH Type I and Type II error rates is:</p>", "expl": "α and β trade off against each other at fixed n; more data improves both."}, {"id": "U8-Q11", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["2.0", "0.5", "10.0", "0"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A die-fairness check: 4 categories each expected 25 times; observed 30, 20, 25, 25. χ² = ?</p>", "expl": "(30−25)²/25 + (20−25)²/25 + 0 + 0 = 1 + 1 = 2.0."}, {"id": "U8-Q12", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["4", "3", "2", "99"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>That goodness-of-fit test's degrees of freedom:</p>", "expl": "df = k − 1 = 3."}, {"id": "U8-Q13", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the same patients' blood pressure before and after medication", "twins assigned one to each diet", "the proportion of men vs women who exercise", "the mean GPA of randomly selected athletes vs randomly selected non-athletes"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Which scenario calls for an INDEPENDENT-samples t-test?</p>", "expl": "Two unrelated groups, comparing MEANS. (A and B are paired; C is proportions.)"}, {"id": "U8-Q14", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.30", "0.20", "0.25", "0.50"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Two-proportion test: 30 of 100 in group 1, 20 of 100 in group 2. The pooled proportion is:</p>", "expl": "Pool everything: (30+20)/(100+100) = 50/200 = 0.25."}, {"id": "U8-Q15", "unit": 8, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the variables are proven independent", "fail to reject H₀ — no evidence of an association", "the variables are associated", "the test must be rerun with more categories"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A chi-square test of independence gives p-value = 0.40. Conclusion:</p>", "expl": "Large p → the data are consistent with independence; that's absence of evidence, not proof."}, {"id": "U9-Q11", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.81", "0.90", "−0.90", "−0.81"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For a regression with r² = 0.81 and a NEGATIVE slope, r = ?</p>", "expl": "r = ±√0.81 = ±0.9; the slope's sign gives the direction → −0.9."}, {"id": "U9-Q12", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["25", "20", "7", "52"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>ŷ = 5 + 2x. The predicted value at x = 10 is:</p>", "expl": "5 + 2(10) = 25."}, {"id": "U9-Q13", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["3", "−3", "47", "0.88"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A point has y = 22 and ŷ = 25. The residual is:</p>", "expl": "Residual = y − ŷ = −3; the point sits below the line. (A reverses the order.)"}, {"id": "U9-Q14", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["is multiplied by 2.54", "is divided by 2.54", "becomes 0", "is unchanged — r has no units"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Heights (x) are converted from centimeters to inches. The correlation r:</p>", "expl": "Correlation is computed from z-scores, so linear unit changes can't touch it."}, {"id": "U9-Q15", "unit": 9, "kind": "bank", "options": ["one point far from the line at an extreme x value", "one point exactly on the line near the center", "a duplicate of an existing point", "re-sorting the rows of the data table"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which addition damages a strong correlation the most?</p>", "expl": "Influential points — extreme in x AND off the pattern — grab the line and crush r."}, {"id": "U5-Q16", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.7887", "0.8944", "0.2113", "0.1056"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Heights ~ N(70, 8). P(60 &lt; X &lt; 80) = ?</p>", "expl": "z runs from −1.25 to 1.25 → 0.8944 − 0.1056 = 0.7887. (B forgets to subtract the left tail.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q17", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.4338", "0.8413", "0.1587", "0.0475"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>SAT scores ~ N(500, 90). For random samples of n = 36, P(x̄ &gt; 515) = ?</p>", "expl": "SE = 90/√36 = 15; z = (515−500)/15 = 1 → 0.1587. (A uses σ = 90, the SD-vs-SE trap.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q18", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.4207", "0.3085", "0.0500", "0.0228"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A fair coin (p = 0.5) is flipped 400 times. P(the sample proportion of heads exceeds 0.55) = ?</p>", "expl": "SE = √(0.5·0.5/400) = 0.025; z = 0.05/0.025 = 2 → 0.0228."}, {"id": "U5-Q19", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["n ≥ 5", "n ≥ 30", "n ≥ 100", "the data are transformed to be symmetric"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A population is heavily skewed. The usual rule of thumb says normal-based methods for x̄ are safe once:</p>", "expl": "The classic CLT cutoff — by n ≈ 30 the sampling distribution of x̄ is approximately normal for most population shapes."}, {"id": "U5-Q20", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["n = 25", "n = 100", "they're all equal — SE depends only on σ", "n = 400"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>σ = 10. Which sample size gives the SMALLEST standard error of x̄?</p>", "expl": "SE = 10/√n: 2.0, 1.0, and 0.5 — bigger samples pin down the mean tighter."}, {"id": "U5-Q21", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.96", "0.975", "1.645", "2.576"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>The z-value with area 0.975 to its LEFT is:</p>", "expl": "That's why 1.96 is the 95% critical value — 2.5% sits in each tail."}, {"id": "U5-Q22", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.0668", "0.0228", "0.9772", "0.1587"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>IQ ~ N(100, 15). P(X &lt; 70) = ?</p>", "expl": "z = (70−100)/15 = −2 → 0.0228. (C is the complement; A uses z = −1.5.)"}, {"id": "U5-Q23", "unit": 5, "kind": "bank", "options": ["fine — the CLT always applies", "fine — 12 exceeds 10", "not safe — with small n you need the population itself to be approximately normal", "impossible at any sample size"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The population's shape is unknown and possibly skewed, and the sample has n = 12. Normal-based inference for x̄ is:</p>", "expl": "The CLT needs large n; for small samples the normality must come from the population."}, {"id": "U6-Q16", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["1.96", "2.576", "1.645", "3.000"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>The critical value z* for a 99% confidence interval is:</p>", "expl": "99% leaves 0.5% per tail → z₀.₉₉₅ ≈ 2.576."}, {"id": "U6-Q17", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["10.2 and 4.4", "12.4 and 4.4", "14.6 and 2.2", "12.4 and 2.2"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A confidence interval for μ is (10.2, 14.6). The point estimate and margin of error are:</p>", "expl": "Center = (10.2+14.6)/2 = 12.4; half-width = 2.2."}, {"id": "U6-Q18", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.049", "0.025", "0.098", "0.0006"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>n = 400 and p̂ = 0.50. The 95% margin of error for p is about:</p>", "expl": "ME = 1.96·√(0.25/400) = 1.96(0.025) ≈ 0.049 — the classic \"±5 points\" of polls. (B forgets z*.)"}, {"id": "U6-Q19", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["smaller", "equal", "larger — t compensates for estimating σ with s", "sometimes smaller, sometimes larger"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For the same confidence level and data, t* compared to z* is always:</p>", "expl": "The t distribution has heavier tails (extra uncertainty from using s), so its critical values exceed z's, shrinking toward z as df grows."}, {"id": "U6-Q20", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["larger", "smaller", "unchanged", "exactly zero"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Keeping the same data but raising the confidence level from 90% to 99% makes the margin of error:</p>", "expl": "Higher confidence demands a bigger critical value → wider net."}, {"id": "U6-Q21", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["fail to reject — 55 is close to the upper bound", "accept H₀", "the CI cannot be used for this", "reject H₀ — 55 lies outside the interval"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A 95% CI for μ is (45.7, 54.3). Testing H₀: μ = 55 vs Hₐ: μ ≠ 55 at α = 0.05:</p>", "expl": "A 95% CI contains exactly the H₀ values that survive a two-tailed α = 0.05 test; 55 doesn't."}, {"id": "U6-Q22", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["96", "25", "97", "10"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>To estimate a mean within E = 3 with 95% confidence when σ = 15, the required n is:</p>", "expl": "n = (1.96·15/3)² = 96.04 → always round UP."}, {"id": "U6-Q23", "unit": 6, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the sample mean must equal the population mean", "a random sample AND (population roughly normal OR n large)", "σ must be known exactly", "the data must contain no variability"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Which set of conditions justifies a t-interval for μ?</p>", "expl": "Random sampling plus normality-or-large-n is the standard checklist; σ known would call for z instead."}, {"id": "U7-Q16", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["t = −0.33", "t = −3.6", "t = −2.0", "t = 0.60"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Test H₀: μ = 8 hours against Hₐ: μ ≠ 8 with x̄ = 7.4, s = 1.8, n = 36. The test statistic is:</p>", "expl": "SE = 1.8/√36 = 0.3; t = (7.4−8)/0.3 = −2.0. (A divides by s; B divides by s/n.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q17", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["35", "36", "34", "6"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>The degrees of freedom for that test are:</p>", "expl": "df = n − 1 = 35."}, {"id": "U7-Q18", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["reject H₀ — it's close enough", "accept H₀ — the mean is exactly 8", "flip a coin — the evidence is perfectly balanced", "fail to reject H₀ — 0.053 exceeds 0.05, even if barely"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>That test's two-tailed p-value is about 0.053. At α = 0.05 the decision is:</p>", "expl": "The rule is mechanical: reject only when p ≤ α. Report the near-miss honestly, but the formal decision stands."}, {"id": "U7-Q19", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["0.9641", "0.0359", "0.0718", "0.1800"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Testing the claim \"fewer than 40% of students commute\" yields z = −1.8. The p-value (left-tailed) is:</p>", "expl": "P(Z &lt; −1.8) = 0.0359. (C doubles it — but this is one-tailed; A is the wrong tail.)"}, {"id": "U7-Q20", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["fail to reject H₀ — insufficient evidence", "accept H₀ — commuting is exactly 40%", "the test is invalid because z is negative", "reject H₀ — sufficient evidence that fewer than 40% commute"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>With that p-value (0.0359) and α = 0.05:</p>", "expl": "0.0359 ≤ 0.05 → reject, and conclude in the claim's direction and context."}, {"id": "U7-Q21", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the test will be wrong 95% of the time", "β must equal 0.95", "there is a 5% chance of wrongly rejecting it (a Type I error)", "the p-value will always be 0.05"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Using α = 0.05 means that IF H₀ is true:</p>", "expl": "α is the Type I error rate, fixed before the data are seen."}, {"id": "U7-Q22", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["also significant — p ≤ 0.01 implies p ≤ 0.05", "not necessarily significant", "significant only if n is large", "exactly borderline"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A result is significant at α = 0.01. At α = 0.05 the same result is:</p>", "expl": "Clearing the stricter bar clears the looser one automatically."}, {"id": "U7-Q23", "unit": 7, "kind": "bank", "options": ["the sample mean is normal", "np₀ ≥ 10 and n(1−p₀) ≥ 10 — using the H₀ value p₀, not p̂", "σ is known", "the population is smaller than the sample"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Before a one-proportion z-test, the condition to verify is:</p>", "expl": "The test is computed under H₀, so the normality check uses p₀."}, {"id": "MX-Q11", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["two-sample independent t-interval", "one-proportion z-interval", "chi-square test of independence", "paired t-interval on the differences"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Estimate the average weight change for 15 people measured before AND after a program. Procedure:</p>", "expl": "Same people, two measurements → dependent samples → analyze the differences."}, {"id": "MX-Q12", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["paired t-test", "two-proportion z-test", "one-way ANOVA", "linear regression"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Does the percent of defective parts differ between two factories (random samples from each)? Procedure:</p>", "expl": "Two independent groups, categorical (defective/not) outcome."}, {"id": "MX-Q13", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["correlation and least-squares regression", "chi-square goodness-of-fit", "two-sample t-test", "one-proportion z-test"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Describe the relationship between hours studied and exam score (both quantitative). Procedure:</p>", "expl": "Two quantitative variables → scatterplot, r, regression line."}, {"id": "MX-Q14", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["one-sample t-test", "two-proportion z-test", "chi-square goodness-of-fit test", "linear regression"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A casino die is rolled 600 times; you record how often each face appeared. To test fairness:</p>", "expl": "One categorical variable against claimed proportions (1/6 each) → GOF, df = 5."}, {"id": "MX-Q15", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["one-sample t-interval", "one-proportion z-interval", "paired t-interval", "chi-square test of independence"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Estimate the percentage of all CCM students who work, from a random sample of 300. Procedure:</p>", "expl": "One categorical yes/no variable, goal = estimate a proportion."}, {"id": "MX-Q16", "unit": 10, "kind": "bank", "options": ["six separate two-sample t-tests", "chi-square test of homogeneity", "two-sample t-test on the largest and smallest", "one-way ANOVA"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Compare the mean customer wait time across FOUR bank branches. Procedure:</p>", "expl": "3+ group means in one analysis; running many t-tests inflates the Type I error rate."}];
const BOSSES = {"1": {"name": "The Biased Pollster", "questions": [{"id": "B1-1", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["nonresponse bias — many members refused", "interviewer bias — the researcher influenced answers", "selection bias — gym members differ systematically from the general population", "response bias — people lie about exercise"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A nutrition researcher recruits 500 volunteers at a gym to study Americans' exercise habits. The main problem is:</p>", "expl": "A convenience sample of gym-goers can't represent Americans, no matter how many volunteer."}, {"id": "B1-2", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["a designed experiment", "an observational study", "a census", "a stratified survey"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Researchers randomly assign 200 farm plots to receive either Fertilizer X or no fertilizer, then compare yields. This is:</p>", "expl": "Random assignment of treatments = experiment; it supports causal conclusions."}, {"id": "B1-3", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["interval", "ratio", "ordinal", "nominal"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>\"ZIP code\" is measured at which level?</p>", "expl": "ZIP codes are numeric labels — no order, no meaningful differences."}, {"id": "B1-4", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the mean GPA of 250 randomly sampled CCM students", "the mean GPA of all 9,000 CCM students", "the proportion of a 400-voter exit poll choosing candidate A", "the median income computed from a census-tract sample"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Which value is a <i>parameter</i>?</p>", "expl": "Parameters describe the entire population (\"all 9,000\"); the others are computed from samples."}, {"id": "B1-5", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["stratified sampling", "systematic sampling", "simple random sampling", "cluster sampling"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A college randomly selects 5 of its 40 course sections and surveys <i>every</i> student in those sections. This is:</p>", "expl": "Whole groups (clusters) are selected and fully surveyed. Stratified would take <i>some</i> students from <i>every</i> section."}, {"id": "B1-6", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["sampling error in the randomization", "nonresponse from dropouts", "the placebo effect and unconscious bias in subjects and evaluators", "confounding due to age and gender"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>In a double-blind drug trial with a placebo group, the placebo and blinding primarily control for:</p>", "expl": "Blinding stops expectations (of patients AND assessors) from contaminating results; randomization handles D."}, {"id": "B1-7", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["stratified sampling", "cluster sampling", "convenience sampling", "systematic sampling"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>To survey opinions, a dean randomly selects 10 students from each of the college's 6 academic divisions. This is:</p>", "expl": "Some members from every group = stratified."}, {"id": "B1-8", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the online poll — 2 million responses dwarf 1,000", "the random sample — random selection beats raw size, which doesn't cure self-selection bias", "both equally — they're both samples", "neither — polls can never be trusted"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>An online poll with 2 million voluntary responses finds 71% oppose a policy. A random sample of 1,000 finds 48% oppose. Which estimate deserves more trust?</p>", "expl": "Bias doesn't shrink with sample size (Literary Digest, 1936)."}, {"id": "B1-9", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["family income/parental involvement, which drives both music lessons and academic support", "the difficulty of the math test", "random chance in who was surveyed", "students' height"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Students who take music lessons have higher math scores. Before concluding music causes math skill, the most likely lurking variable to consider is:</p>", "expl": "A common cause explains the association without any music→math effect."}, {"id": "B1-10", "unit": 1, "kind": "boss", "options": ["number of texts sent today", "number of siblings", "shoe size as sold (6, 6.5, 7, …)", "the exact volume of water in a bottle"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Which variable is continuous?</p>", "expl": "Measured quantities on a continuum are continuous; counts and fixed step-sizes are discrete."}]}, "2": {"name": "The Outlier", "questions": [{"id": "B2-1", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["mean ≈ 13, median ≈ 18.1", "mean ≈ 18.1, median = 13 — the outlier inflates the mean", "mean = median = 13", "mean ≈ 18.1, median = 14"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>For the data 12, 15, 11, 14, 50, 13, 12 — which statement is true?</p>", "expl": "Mean = 127/7 ≈ 18.14; sorted middle value = 13. The 50 drags only the mean."}, {"id": "B2-2", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["2.83", "10.00", "8.00", "3.16"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The sample standard deviation of 10, 14, 12, 8, 16 is:</p>", "expl": "Mean 12; squared deviations sum to 40; s = √(40/4) = √10 ≈ 3.16. (A divides by n; B is the variance; C is the range.)"}, {"id": "B2-3", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["yes — z = (88−75)/6 ≈ 2.17, which exceeds 2", "no — 88 is only 13 points above average", "no — z ≈ 1.84", "yes — any score above 85 is unusual"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Exam: mean 75, SD 6. Is a score of 88 unusual by the z-score criterion (|z| &gt; 2)?</p>", "expl": "z = 13/6 ≈ 2.17 &gt; 2 → unusual by the criterion."}, {"id": "B2-4", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["41", "12", "45", "11"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Q1 = 22 and Q3 = 30. Which value is an outlier by the 1.5×IQR rule?</p>", "expl": "IQR = 8; fences = 22 − 12 = 10 and 30 + 12 = 42. Only 45 is outside."}, {"id": "B2-5", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["95%", "68%", "99.7%", "75%"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>SAT-style scores are bell-shaped: mean 500, SD 100. About what percent fall between 300 and 700?</p>", "expl": "300–700 is μ ± 2σ → ≈95% by the Empirical Rule."}, {"id": "B2-6", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["skewed left", "skewed right", "symmetric", "uniform"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A distribution has median 13 and mean 18. Its shape is most likely:</p>", "expl": "Mean dragged above the median = long right tail."}, {"id": "B2-7", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["mean and standard deviation", "mean and range", "midrange and variance", "median and IQR"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The best summary measures for strongly skewed home-price data are:</p>", "expl": "Resistant measures for skewed data."}, {"id": "B2-8", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["data set II — its peak is taller", "neither — same range means same SD", "data set I — its values sit farther from the center on average", "it cannot be determined"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Data set I is uniform (flat histogram) over 0–100; data set II is tightly peaked at 50 over the same axis. The larger standard deviation belongs to:</p>", "expl": "SD measures horizontal spread from the mean, not bar-height variation."}, {"id": "B2-9", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the average wait is 12 minutes", "90% of patients wait more than 12 minutes", "waits are 90% accurate to within 12 minutes", "about 90% of patients wait 12 minutes or less"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The 90th percentile of ER wait times is 12 minutes. This means:</p>", "expl": "Percentile = position: 90% at or below."}, {"id": "B2-10", "unit": 2, "kind": "boss", "options": ["decrease the mean substantially but barely move the median", "decrease both mean and median substantially", "decrease the median substantially but not the mean", "leave both unchanged"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Removing the value 50 from the data in B2-1 will:</p>", "expl": "The mean uses magnitudes; the median only order."}]}, "3": {"name": "The Gambler", "questions": [{"id": "B3-1", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.50", "0.34", "0.68", "0.57"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p><i>Use this table for B3-1 and B3-2. Drink preference, 150 students:</i></p><table><tr><th></th><th>Coffee</th><th>Tea</th><th>Neither</th><th>Total</th></tr><tr><td>Male</td><td>24</td><td>21</td><td>15</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>Female</td><td>51</td><td>29</td><td>10</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td>75</td><td>50</td><td>25</td><td>150</td></tr></table><p>P(prefers coffee | female) = ?</p>", "expl": "51/90 ≈ 0.567. (C is P(female | coffee) = 51/75 — the inverted conditional; B is the joint 51/150; A is the marginal 75/150.)"}, {"id": "B3-2", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.57", "0.68", "0.34", "0.60"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>P(female | prefers coffee) = ?</p>", "expl": "51/75 = 0.68. Note it differs from B3-1 — the given condition changes the denominator."}, {"id": "B3-3", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["mutually exclusive", "dependent", "independent, because (0.4)(0.25) = 0.10", "complementary"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>P(A) = 0.4, P(B) = 0.25, P(A and B) = 0.10. The events are:</p>", "expl": "P(A)·P(B) = P(A and B) is the definition of independence."}, {"id": "B3-4", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.55", "0.65", "0.10", "0.75"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>With the same values, P(A or B) = ?</p>", "expl": "0.4 + 0.25 − 0.10 = 0.55. (B forgets the overlap.)"}, {"id": "B3-5", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.05", "0.857", "0.143", "0.15"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Three components each work with probability 0.95, independently. P(at least one fails) = ?</p>", "expl": "1 − 0.95³ = 1 − 0.857 = 0.143. (B stops at P(none fail); D adds 0.05 three times.)"}, {"id": "B3-6", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["48", "11,880", "20,736", "495"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A 12-member club selects a 4-person committee. The number of possible committees is:</p>", "expl": "12C4 = 495. (B is 12P4 — counts orderings; C is 12⁴.)"}, {"id": "B3-7", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["more likely — black is overdue", "exactly as likely as it always was", "less likely — the wheel is streaking red", "certain within the next few spins"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A roulette wheel lands on red 8 spins in a row. On the next spin, black is:</p>", "expl": "Independent spins have no memory."}, {"id": "B3-8", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0", "0.135", "0.75", "cannot be determined"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A and B are mutually exclusive with P(A) = 0.30, P(B) = 0.45. P(A and B) = ?</p>", "expl": "Mutually exclusive MEANS they can't both occur. (B multiplies as if independent — disjoint events with nonzero probabilities are never independent.)"}, {"id": "B3-9", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the casino adjusts odds when losing", "wins and losses must exactly alternate eventually", "each gambler's luck cancels another's", "the law of large numbers — long-run proportions converge to the true probabilities"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Why do casino profits become highly predictable over millions of plays even though each play is random?</p>", "expl": "Relative frequencies stabilize at the expected values over many trials."}, {"id": "B3-10", "unit": 3, "kind": "boss", "options": ["sum of 2 — snake eyes is a special roll", "sum of 7 — six combinations produce it versus one for a sum of 2", "equally likely — every sum has the same chance", "impossible to say without rolling"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Rolling two dice, which is more likely: a sum of 7 or a sum of 2?</p>", "expl": "P(7) = 6/36; P(2) = 1/36. Count the underlying outcomes."}]}, "4": {"name": "Lord Binomial", "questions": [{"id": "B4-1", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["$100", "$300", "−$200", "$200"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>An insurer sells a $300 policy that pays out $10,000 with probability 0.02. The expected profit per policy is:</p>", "expl": "300 − (10,000)(0.02) = 300 − 200 = $100."}, {"id": "B4-2", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.0102", "0.1009", "0.2270", "0.7730"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>X ~ binomial, n = 12, p = 0.4. P(X = 5) = ?</p>", "expl": "12C5(0.4)⁵(0.6)⁷ ≈ 0.227. (A omits 12C5; B swaps p and q; D is the complement of the answer.) TI-84: <code>binompdf(12,.4,5)</code>."}, {"id": "B4-3", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["6.0", "4.8", "2.88", "5.0"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>For that variable, the mean of X is:</p>", "expl": "μ = np = 12(0.4) = 4.8."}, {"id": "B4-4", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["2.88", "4.80", "2.19", "1.70"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>And the standard deviation of X is:</p>", "expl": "σ = √(npq) = √2.88 ≈ 1.697. (A is the variance; C is √4.8.)"}, {"id": "B4-5", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.6561", "0.3439", "0.4000", "0.1000"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Four independent shots each hit with probability 0.1. P(at least one hit) = ?</p>", "expl": "1 − 0.9⁴ = 0.3439. (A is P(no hits); C is 4 × 0.1.)"}, {"id": "B4-6", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the trials aren't independent — p changes as cards leave the deck", "there are more than two outcomes per draw", "n isn't fixed", "hearts are too rare for the binomial model"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Drawing 5 cards from a deck <i>without replacement</i> and counting hearts is NOT binomial because:</p>", "expl": "Without replacement breaks independence/constant p."}, {"id": "B4-7", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.3, 0.3, 0.3, 0.3", "0.5, 0.3, 0.3, −0.1", "0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1", "0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which is a valid probability distribution for X = 1, 2, 3, 4?</p>", "expl": "Sums to exactly 1 with all values in [0,1]. (A sums to 1.2; B has a negative; D sums to 1.1.)"}, {"id": "B4-8", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["every play loses a quarter", "you cannot win on any given play", "after 4 plays you will be down exactly $1", "over many plays, losses average about 25 cents per play"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>A game's expected value is −$0.25 per play. The correct reading is:</p>", "expl": "Expected value is the long-run per-play average."}, {"id": "B4-9", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the number of customers entering a store in an hour", "the time between customer arrivals", "the weight of a randomly chosen customer's purchase", "the temperature in the store at noon"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which is a discrete random variable?</p>", "expl": "Counts are discrete; times, weights, temperatures are continuous."}, {"id": "B4-10", "unit": 4, "kind": "boss", "options": ["typical — within the usual range", "impossible for a fair coin", "unusual — 230 exceeds μ + 2σ = 220, suggesting the coin may not be fair", "unusual only if exactly 230 was predicted in advance"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A fair coin is flipped 400 times: μ = 200 heads, σ = 10. Getting 230 heads would be:</p>", "expl": "230 is 3 SDs above the mean — strong evidence against fairness."}]}, "5": {"name": "The Central Limit Thief", "questions": [{"id": "B5-1", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.9332", "0.1500", "0.0668", "0.4332"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Adult male heights ~ N(70, 8) (inches). P(a random man is shorter than 58 in) = ?</p>", "expl": "z = (58−70)/8 = −1.5 → 0.0668. TI-84: <code>normalcdf(-1E99,58,70,8)</code>."}, {"id": "B5-2", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["56.84", "72.60", "78.40", "83.16"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>For N(70, 8), the cutoff for the tallest 5% is:</p>", "expl": "z₀.₉₅ = 1.645 → 70 + 1.645(8) ≈ 83.2. (A is the <i>shortest</i> 5% cutoff.) TI-84: <code>invNorm(0.95,70,8)</code>."}, {"id": "B5-3", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the sampling distribution of x̄ is approximately normal, whatever the population's shape", "the population becomes approximately normal", "the sample's histogram becomes approximately normal", "all variables in the study become normal"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>The Central Limit Theorem guarantees that for large n:</p>", "expl": "Only the distribution of the sample MEAN normalizes; sample data mirror the population."}, {"id": "B5-4", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["8.00", "1.00", "0.125", "2.83"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>σ = 8 and n = 64. The standard error of x̄ is:</p>", "expl": "8/√64 = 1. (A forgets to divide — the classic SD-vs-SE trap; C divides by n.)"}, {"id": "B5-5", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.4013", "0.1056", "0.5987", "0.0228"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>For that setting (μ = 70, SE = 1), P(x̄ &gt; 72) = ?</p>", "expl": "z = (72−70)/1 = 2 → 0.0228. (A used σ = 8 instead of the SE, giving z = 0.25 — the #1 CLT exam error.)"}, {"id": "B5-6", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.6826", "0.9104", "0.7745", "0.8413"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Still N(70, 8), one individual: P(58 &lt; x &lt; 78) = ?</p>", "expl": "z from −1.5 to 1.0 → 0.8413 − 0.0668 = 0.7745."}, {"id": "B5-7", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["nothing — SE depends only on σ", "cutting it in half (√25 = 5 → √100 = 10)", "cutting it to one quarter", "doubling it"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Increasing the sample size from 25 to 100 changes the standard error by:</p>", "expl": "SE = σ/√n; quadrupling n halves SE."}, {"id": "B5-8", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the small clinic — smaller samples swing more around 50%", "the large hospital — more births, more chances", "both equally — P(girl) is the same everywhere", "neither — that percentage is essentially impossible"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A small clinic delivers ~10 babies/day; a large hospital ~50/day. Over a year, more days with over 70% girls will occur at:</p>", "expl": "Sampling variability shrinks with n; small n → extreme days more often."}, {"id": "B5-9", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.24", "0.0024", "0.049", "0.40"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>For p = 0.40 and n = 100, the standard error of the sample proportion p̂ is:</p>", "expl": "√(0.4·0.6/100) = √0.0024 ≈ 0.049. (A is pq; B forgets the square root.)"}, {"id": "B5-10", "unit": 5, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the population distribution", "the sample's histogram", "the range of the raw data", "the sampling distribution of x̄"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>As n grows, which ONE of these gets narrower?</p>", "expl": "The population is fixed; the sample increasingly resembles it; only the distribution of x̄ tightens (σ/√n)."}]}, "6": {"name": "The Interval Keeper", "questions": [{"id": "B6-1", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["(46.08, 53.92)", "(45.74, 54.26)", "(48.93, 51.07)", "(32.95, 67.05)"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>n = 16, x̄ = 50, s = 8. The 95% CI for μ (t* = 2.131, df = 15) is:</p>", "expl": "SE = 8/√16 = 2; ME = 2.131 × 2 = 4.26. (A used z* = 1.96; C used s/n = 0.5; D used s without √n.) TI-84: <code>TInterval</code>."}, {"id": "B6-2", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the population standard deviation σ is unknown — we estimated it with s", "the sample size is below 30", "the data are skewed", "t is always more accurate than z"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Why t* and not z* in B6-1?</p>", "expl": "The t distribution compensates for estimating σ — that's the rule, at any n. (B is the shortcut misconception.)"}, {"id": "B6-3", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["(0.625, 0.695)", "(0.606, 0.714)", "(0.660, 0.726)", "(0.594, 0.726)"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>132 of 200 customers prefer the new recipe. The 95% CI for p is:</p>", "expl": "p̂ = 0.66; SE = √(0.66·0.34/200) ≈ 0.0335; ME = 1.96(0.0335) ≈ 0.066. TI-84: <code>1-PropZInt</code>."}, {"id": "B6-4", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["95% of customers fall inside the interval", "there is a 95% chance p is between 0.594 and 0.726", "the method used produces intervals that capture the true proportion in about 95% of all samples", "95% of future samples will give exactly this interval"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The correct interpretation of \"95% confident\" for the interval in B6-3 is:</p>", "expl": "The confidence describes the procedure's long-run success rate, not this one interval or the data."}, {"id": "B6-5", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["423", "422", "601", "26"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>To estimate a proportion within E = 0.04 at 90% confidence with no prior estimate, the required n is:</p>", "expl": "n = (1.645)²(0.25)/(0.04)² = 422.8 → round UP. (B rounds down; C used z* = 1.96.)"}, {"id": "B6-6", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["a 95% CI compared to a 99% CI", "a 99% CI compared to a 95% CI", "an interval from a larger sample", "an interval with a smaller s"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Which interval is WIDER, all else equal?</p>", "expl": "More confidence demands a wider net."}, {"id": "B6-7", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["50 and 8.52", "45.74 and 8.52", "50 and 4.26", "54.26 and 4.26"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>From the interval (45.74, 54.26), the point estimate and margin of error are:</p>", "expl": "Center = midpoint; ME = half-width."}, {"id": "B6-8", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["p has a 95% probability of being inside it", "p moves in and out of the interval from sample to sample", "the interval will contain 95% of customer responses", "the interval either captures p or it doesn't — nothing about it is random anymore"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Once the interval (0.594, 0.726) is computed, which is true?</p>", "expl": "Probability attaches to the method before sampling, not to a computed interval."}, {"id": "B6-9", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the population must be normal", "np̂ ≥ 10 and n(1−p̂) ≥ 10 (and a random sample)", "n must exceed 100", "p̂ must be between 0.3 and 0.7"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Before building a proportion CI, the condition to check is:</p>", "expl": "Enough successes AND failures for the normal approximation."}, {"id": "B6-10", "unit": 6, "kind": "boss", "options": ["cutting it in half", "cutting it to one quarter", "doubling it", "nothing predictable"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Quadrupling the sample size (with the same confidence level) changes the margin of error by:</p>", "expl": "ME ∝ 1/√n."}]}, "7": {"name": "The Null Hypothesis", "questions": [{"id": "B7-1", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["H₀: p = 0.25 vs Hₐ: p ≠ 0.25", "H₀: p &lt; 0.25 vs Hₐ: p = 0.25", "H₀: p̂ = 0.25 vs Hₐ: p̂ &lt; 0.25", "H₀: p = 0.25 vs Hₐ: p &lt; 0.25"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Claim to test: \"Fewer than 25% of students commute by bus.\" The hypotheses are:</p>", "expl": "\"Fewer than\" → left-tailed alternative; H₀ holds the equality; parameters (p), never statistics (p̂)."}, {"id": "B7-2", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["t = 0.33", "t = 12.0", "t = 2.0", "t = 4.0"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Test H₀: μ = 100 with x̄ = 104, s = 12, n = 36. The test statistic is:</p>", "expl": "t = (104−100)/(12/√36) = 4/2 = 2.0, df = 35."}, {"id": "B7-3", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["fail to reject H₀ — the evidence falls just short of significance (which is not proof that μ = 100)", "reject H₀ — 0.053 is close enough to 0.05", "accept H₀ — the mean is exactly 100", "the test is inconclusive and must be thrown out"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>That test's two-tailed p-value is 0.053. At α = 0.05:</p>", "expl": "Reject only when p ≤ α; and \"fail to reject\" never means \"proved H₀.\""}, {"id": "B7-4", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["there's a 5.3% chance H₀ is true", "if μ really were 100, samples this extreme would occur about 5.3% of the time", "the result will replicate 94.7% of the time", "5.3% of the data are outliers"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A p-value of 0.053 means:</p>", "expl": "P(data this extreme | H₀ true). Never a probability about H₀ itself."}, {"id": "B7-5", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["z = 0.06", "z = 3.0", "z = 1.5", "z = 0.66"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Claim: \"More than 60% of members renew.\" n = 150, p̂ = 0.66. The test statistic is:</p>", "expl": "SE = √(0.6·0.4/150) = 0.04; z = (0.66−0.60)/0.04 = 1.5. TI-84: <code>1-PropZTest</code>."}, {"id": "B7-6", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.9332", "0.1336", "0.0500", "0.0668"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The right-tailed p-value for z = 1.5 is:</p>", "expl": "1 − 0.9332 = 0.0668. (B is the two-tailed value — read the claim's direction.)"}, {"id": "B7-7", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["reject H₀ — 66% is clearly more than 60%", "fail to reject H₀ — insufficient evidence that more than 60% renew (p = 0.0668 &gt; 0.05)", "accept H₀ — exactly 60% renew", "reject Hₐ and adopt a larger sample"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>At α = 0.05, the conclusion for B7-5/B7-6 is:</p>", "expl": "The sample difference wasn't large enough relative to sampling variability."}, {"id": "B7-8", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["concluding the therapy doesn't work when it actually does", "concluding the therapy works when it actually doesn't", "setting β too low", "rejecting both hypotheses"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>H₀: the new therapy has no effect. A Type II error is:</p>", "expl": "Type II = failing to detect a real effect (a miss); B is Type I (false alarm)."}, {"id": "B7-9", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["requires stronger evidence to reject H₀ — fewer Type I errors but more Type II errors", "makes rejecting H₀ easier", "reduces both error types simultaneously", "changes nothing about the test's behavior"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Moving α from 0.05 to 0.01:</p>", "expl": "The error types trade off; only larger n improves both."}, {"id": "B7-10", "unit": 7, "kind": "boss", "options": ["practically and statistically significant", "statistically insignificant because 11 steps is tiny", "statistically significant but practically trivial — the huge n detected a meaningless difference", "evidence the app meaningfully improves fitness"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A study of 1.5 million users finds an app increases daily steps by 11 steps, p &lt; 0.0001. The best description:</p>", "expl": "Significance measures detectability, not importance."}]}, "8": {"name": "The Chi-Square Sphinx", "questions": [{"id": "B8-1", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["24", "27", "85", "170"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>In a test of independence, a cell's row total is 80, column total 90, grand total 300. Its expected count is:</p>", "expl": "E = (80)(90)/300 = 24."}, {"id": "B8-2", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["12", "6", "11", "7"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A contingency table has 4 rows and 3 columns. The degrees of freedom are:</p>", "expl": "(4−1)(3−1) = 6."}, {"id": "B8-3", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the observed counts equal the expected counts", "at least one proportion differs", "the variables are associated", "the two variables are independent in the population"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The null hypothesis of a chi-square test of independence states:</p>", "expl": "Chi-square nulls are always the \"no relationship\" claim; rejection = evidence of association."}, {"id": "B8-4", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["two-sample t with independent samples", "two-proportion z-test", "paired t-test on the differences", "chi-square goodness-of-fit"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Each employee's output is measured before and after ergonomic training. The right procedure is:</p>", "expl": "Same subjects twice → dependent samples."}, {"id": "B8-5", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["a paired t-test", "one-way ANOVA", "a one-sample z-test", "a two-proportion z-test"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Comparing the <i>proportion</i> of freshmen vs seniors who own cars requires:</p>", "expl": "Two independent groups, categorical outcome."}, {"id": "B8-6", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["group 1's mean is significantly greater — zero isn't a plausible difference", "no significant difference exists", "group 2's mean is significantly greater", "the interval is invalid because it excludes zero"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>A 95% CI for μ₁ − μ₂ is (0.8, 4.2). At α = 0.05:</p>", "expl": "Zero lies outside, and the whole interval is positive → μ₁ &gt; μ₂."}, {"id": "B8-7", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the variables are strongly associated", "observed counts are close to what independence predicts — no evidence against H₀", "the sample size was too small", "a calculation error occurred — χ² can't be near zero"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A chi-square statistic near zero means:</p>", "expl": "χ² = Σ(O−E)²/E grows with discrepancy; small χ² = good agreement with independence."}, {"id": "B8-8", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["119", "6", "5", "120"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>A die is rolled 120 times to test whether it's fair (6 categories). The goodness-of-fit df is:</p>", "expl": "df = k − 1 = 5."}, {"id": "B8-9", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["twins split between two diets", "30 randomly chosen users of phone A vs 32 randomly chosen users of phone B, comparing mean battery life", "the same students tested before and after a course", "one sample compared against a known standard"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Which scenario calls for an INDEPENDENT two-sample t-test?</p>", "expl": "Two unrelated random groups; A and C are paired designs."}, {"id": "B8-10", "unit": 8, "kind": "boss", "options": ["four separate t-tests", "a chi-square test of independence", "a two-proportion z-test", "one-way ANOVA"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Comparing mean test scores across FOUR teaching methods in one analysis requires:</p>", "expl": "ANOVA tests 3+ means at once without inflating Type I error."}]}, "9": {"name": "The Extrapolator", "questions": [{"id": "B9-1", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["−0.35", "−0.7", "−1.4", "−2.0"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p><i>Setup for B9-1 to B9-4: hours of TV per week (x) vs exam score (y). r = −0.7, s_y = 10, s_x = 5, ȳ = 40, x̄ = 10.</i> The least-squares slope is:</p>", "expl": "b = r(s_y/s_x) = −0.7(10/5) = −1.4. (A inverts the ratio.)"}, {"id": "B9-2", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["26", "40", "14", "54"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The intercept is:</p>", "expl": "a = ȳ − b·x̄ = 40 − (−1.4)(10) = 54."}, {"id": "B9-3", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["70.8", "37.2", "52.6", "16.8"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>The predicted score for a student watching 12 hours is:</p>", "expl": "ŷ = 54 − 1.4(12) = 37.2."}, {"id": "B9-4", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["−2.2, the point lies below the line", "+2.2, the point lies above the line", "−2.2, the point lies above the line", "+2.2, the point lies below the line"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>That student actually scored 35. The residual is:</p>", "expl": "Residual = y − ŷ = 35 − 37.2 = −2.2; negative = observed below predicted."}, {"id": "B9-5", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["70%", "49%", "−49%", "30%"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>With r = −0.7, the percent of variation in scores explained by the linear relationship with TV hours is:</p>", "expl": "r² = 0.49. (A reads r as a percent; C — r² can't be negative.)"}, {"id": "B9-6", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["each TV hour causes a 1.4-point score drop", "every student who watches one more hour scores exactly 1.4 points lower", "each additional weekly TV hour is associated with a predicted score about 1.4 points lower, on average", "scores fall 1.4% per TV hour"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The best interpretation of the slope −1.4:</p>", "expl": "Predicted/average language only — no causation (observational), no determinism."}, {"id": "B9-7", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["valid because the line extends forever", "valid if r is strong enough", "valid as an average prediction", "unreliable extrapolation — the linear pattern is unverified beyond the data"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>The data covered 5–15 TV hours. Predicting the score of someone watching 40 hours is:</p>", "expl": "Predictions are only supported within the observed x-range."}, {"id": "B9-8", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["this is observational data — confounders (e.g., study time) could drive both variables", "the correlation is negative", "the sample came from one college", "p-values can't be that small"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Even with p &lt; 0.001 for this relationship, concluding \"TV watching lowers exam scores\" is unjustified because:</p>", "expl": "Significance rules out chance, not confounding. Causation needs random assignment."}, {"id": "B9-9", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["weak and negative", "strong and curved", "moderately strong, positive, linear", "moderately strong, negative, linear"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>r = −0.7 describes a relationship that is:</p>", "expl": "Sign = direction; |0.7| = moderately strong; r only measures LINEAR association."}, {"id": "B9-10", "unit": 9, "kind": "boss", "options": ["leave the line unchanged — one point can't matter", "substantially pull the slope toward itself (an influential point)", "change the intercept but never the slope", "increase r toward +1 automatically"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>One added point far to the right of all the others, well off the line's trend, will most likely:</p>", "expl": "Points extreme in x have high leverage on the fitted line."}]}, "10": {"name": "The Final Exam Dragon", "questions": [{"id": "DR-1", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["t-interval for a mean", "one-proportion z-interval", "z-interval with σ known", "chi-square goodness-of-fit"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Estimate the mean coffee spending of all students from a random sample of 40 (σ unknown). Procedure:</p>", "expl": "Estimating a MEAN with σ unknown → t-interval, df = 39."}, {"id": "DR-2", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["σ", "p", "p̂", "μ"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Which symbol is a statistic?</p>", "expl": "Hats/Latin letters from samples; Greek/plain p for populations."}, {"id": "DR-3", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["cluster sampling", "stratified sampling", "convenience sampling", "systematic sampling"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>Inspecting every 12th item off an assembly line is:</p>", "expl": "Selecting every kth item is systematic sampling."}, {"id": "DR-4", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["test 2 — the higher raw score", "test 1 — z = 2.0 beats z = 1.0", "equal — both above the mean", "test 2 — it had more spread"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Ana scored 84 on test 1 (mean 76, SD 4) and 88 on test 2 (mean 82, SD 6). Her relatively better performance was:</p>", "expl": "(84−76)/4 = 2.0 vs (88−82)/6 = 1.0."}, {"id": "DR-5", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.65", "0.75", "0.10", "0.55"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>P(pass math) = 0.40, P(pass stats) = 0.25, P(pass both) = 0.10. P(pass at least one) = ?</p>", "expl": "0.40 + 0.25 − 0.10 = 0.55."}, {"id": "DR-6", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.3439", "0.6561", "0.4000", "0.9000"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Four independent free throws, each made with probability 0.1 (a struggling shooter). P(makes at least one) = ?</p>", "expl": "1 − 0.9⁴."}, {"id": "DR-7", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["56.84", "78.00", "83.16", "86.45"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Heights ~ N(70, 8). The 95th percentile is:</p>", "expl": "70 + 1.645(8)."}, {"id": "DR-8", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.4013", "0.0228", "0.5987", "0.1056"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Same population, samples of n = 64: P(x̄ &gt; 72) = ?</p>", "expl": "SE = 1, z = 2. (A is the σ-instead-of-SE trap.)"}, {"id": "DR-9", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["the procedure that built it captures the parameter in about 95% of all possible samples", "it contains 95% of the population's individual values", "the parameter has a 95% probability of being inside this specific interval", "95% of sample means from future studies will land inside it"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which statement about a 95% confidence interval is TRUE?</p>", "expl": "The 95% describes the method’s long-run capture rate — not this one interval, not the data, not future sample means."}, {"id": "DR-10", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["(46.08, 53.92)", "(48.93, 51.07)", "(42.00, 58.00)", "(45.74, 54.26)"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>n = 16, x̄ = 50, s = 8, t* = 2.131. The 95% CI for μ:</p>", "expl": "50 ± 2.131(2)."}, {"id": "DR-11", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["p ≠ 0.60", "p &lt; 0.60", "p &gt; 0.60", "p ≥ 0.60"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>Claim: \"More than 60% of customers would recommend us.\" Hₐ is:</p>", "expl": "“More than” → right-tailed alternative, stated about the parameter p."}, {"id": "DR-12", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["reject H₀ — there is sufficient evidence for the claim", "fail to reject H₀ — there is not sufficient evidence that more than 60% recommend", "accept H₀ — exactly 60% recommend", "lower α until the result is significant"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>That test gives p-value = 0.0668 at α = 0.05. Conclusion:</p>", "expl": "p-value 0.0668 &gt; α = 0.05 → insufficient evidence for the claim; and we never “accept” H₀."}, {"id": "DR-13", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["failing to adopt a system that works", "the study was underpowered", "correctly detecting an effect", "concluding the system works when it actually does nothing"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>H₀: the alarm system has no effect on break-ins. A Type I error means:</p>", "expl": "Rejecting a true H₀ = false positive."}, {"id": "DR-14", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["24", "27", "56.7", "7,200"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Row total 80, column total 90, n = 300: the expected cell count is:</p>", "expl": "E = (row total)(column total)/(grand total) = 80 × 90/300 = 24."}, {"id": "DR-15", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["two-sample independent t-test", "paired t-test", "two-proportion z-test", "chi-square test"], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>Same 12 athletes timed before and after a training program. Compare means with:</p>", "expl": "Same subjects measured twice → dependent samples → paired t-test on the differences."}, {"id": "DR-16", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["scores drop exactly 1.4 points per hour for every student", "TV causes a 1.4-point drop per hour", "each extra TV hour predicts a score about 1.4 points lower, on average", "1.4% of scores are explained by TV"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>ŷ = 54 − 1.4x (score from TV hours). The −1.4 means:</p>", "expl": "Slope interpretation must use predicted/average language — never deterministic (B-style) or causal (observational data)."}, {"id": "DR-17", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["0.70", "0.30", "−0.49", "0.49"], "answer": 3, "stem": "<p>r = −0.7. The proportion of variation in y explained by x is:</p>", "expl": "r² = (−0.7)² = 0.49 — the sign disappears when squaring; r² can never be negative."}, {"id": "DR-18", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["a randomized experiment assigning users to app vs no-app and comparing sleep", "a survey of 1 million app users reporting good sleep", "a strong correlation between app downloads and sleep scores", "expert testimony from the app's developers"], "answer": 0, "stem": "<p>Which study design would justify the headline \"New app CAUSES better sleep\"?</p>", "expl": "Only random assignment justifies causal language; sample size and correlation strength cannot."}, {"id": "DR-19", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["individual observations", "the population", "the sample mean from sample to sample", "the sample standard deviation"], "answer": 2, "stem": "<p>The quantity σ/√n describes the variability of:</p>", "expl": "That's the standard error — the Dragon's favorite trap."}, {"id": "DR-20", "unit": 10, "kind": "boss", "options": ["\"We reject H₀\" — 0.41 is a large test statistic", "\"We fail to reject H₀ — the data do not provide sufficient evidence of a change,\" which is weaker than proof", "the sentence is fine as written", "\"The probability H₀ is true is 41%\""], "answer": 1, "stem": "<p>A test yields p-value = 0.41. The student writes: \"We accept H₀ and have proven the mean is unchanged.\" The correct fix:</p>", "expl": "Tests never prove the null; they only measure evidence against it."}]}};
const DIAG = [{"id": "D1", "skill": "percents", "stem": "<p>Write 0.3% as a decimal.</p>", "options": ["0.03", "0.003", "0.3", "0.0003"], "answer": 1, "expl": "Percent means ÷100: 0.3% = 0.3/100 = 0.003. This exact conversion shows up in every p-value and probability."}, {"id": "D2", "skill": "percents", "stem": "<p>A lottery has a 1% chance of winning per ticket. About how many winners among 1,000 players?</p>", "options": ["1", "100", "50", "10"], "answer": 3, "expl": "1% of 1,000 = 0.01 × 1000 = 10. (Only ~80% of highly-educated adults get this one right.)"}, {"id": "D3", "skill": "fractions", "stem": "<p>Which is larger: 4/7 or 5/8?</p>", "options": ["5/8", "4/7", "they are equal", "impossible to tell without a calculator"], "answer": 0, "expl": "5/8 = 0.625 vs 4/7 ≈ 0.571. Community-college students get fraction comparisons right only ~70% of the time — chance is 50%."}, {"id": "D4", "skill": "fractions", "stem": "<p>Compute 1/2 ÷ 2/3.</p>", "options": ["1/3", "3", "3/4", "4/3"], "answer": 2, "expl": "Dividing by a fraction = multiply by its reciprocal: (1/2)(3/2) = 3/4. 38% of intro-stats students miss this exact item. (1/3 is the multiply-instead trap.)"}, {"id": "D5", "skill": "decimals", "stem": "<p>True or false: 0.049 &lt; 0.05.</p>", "options": ["true", "false", "they are equal", "it depends on rounding"], "answer": 0, "expl": "0.049 < 0.050. This IS the p-value-vs-α comparison — the single most consequential decimal judgment in the course."}, {"id": "D6", "skill": "negatives", "stem": "<p>−3² equals:</p>", "options": ["9", "−9", "6", "−6"], "answer": 1, "expl": "Exponents come before negation: −3² = −(3²) = −9, while (−3)² = 9. The TI-84 follows this rule and it's a documented top calculator error."}, {"id": "D7", "skill": "parentheses", "stem": "<p>Compute 12 ÷ (6 ÷ 36).</p>", "options": ["0.056", "2", "18", "72"], "answer": 3, "expl": "Inside first: 6/36 = 1/6, and 12 ÷ (1/6) = 72. Typed without parentheses, 12/6/36 = 0.056 — the classic stats-formula calculator disaster (it's the t-statistic error in disguise)."}, {"id": "D8", "skill": "formulas", "stem": "<p>Compute z = (68 − 74)/4.</p>", "options": ["1.5", "−6", "−1.5", "0.67"], "answer": 2, "expl": "(68−74) = −6, then −6/4 = −1.5. Negative z just means below the mean — you'll compute this a hundred times in Unit 5."}, {"id": "D9", "skill": "equations", "stem": "<p>Solve for x: 1.96 = (x − 100)/15.</p>", "options": ["101.96", "129.4", "70.6", "96.1"], "answer": 1, "expl": "Multiply both sides by 15: 29.4 = x − 100, so x = 129.4. This is literally “solve the z-formula for x” — the only algebra move intro stats demands."}, {"id": "D10", "skill": "formulas", "stem": "<p>For the data {2, 4, 9}, the sample standard deviation s = √(Σ(x−x̄)²/(n−1)) is closest to:</p>", "options": ["√13 ≈ 3.61", "√8.67 ≈ 2.94", "13", "26"], "answer": 0, "expl": "x̄ = 5; deviations −3, −1, 4; squares 9+1+16 = 26; 26/(3−1) = 13; s = √13 ≈ 3.61. (2.94 divides by n instead of n−1.) This one item rehearses ~80% of the course's hand computation."}, {"id": "D11", "skill": "roots", "stem": "<p>√40 lies between which two whole numbers?</p>", "options": ["4 and 5", "5 and 6", "20 and 21", "6 and 7"], "answer": 3, "expl": "6² = 36 and 7² = 49, so √40 is between 6 and 7 (≈6.32). Estimating roots was one of the five skills that significantly predicted stats grades."}, {"id": "D12", "skill": "formulas", "stem": "<p>SE = σ/√n with σ = 20 and n = 25. SE = ?</p>", "options": ["0.8", "100", "4", "20"], "answer": 2, "expl": "√25 = 5, then 20/5 = 4. (0.8 divides by n; 20 forgets to divide at all — the future SD-vs-SE trap.)"}, {"id": "D13", "skill": "notation", "stem": "<p>Your calculator displays <code>2.3E-4</code>. As a decimal, that is:</p>", "options": ["2.30004", "0.00023", "23000", "0.0023"], "answer": 1, "expl": "E-4 means ×10⁻⁴: move the decimal 4 places left → 0.00023. Misreading E-notation as ≈2.3 is a documented exam-point killer for tiny p-values."}, {"id": "D14", "skill": "percents", "stem": "<p>40 of 250 survey respondents said yes. The proportion who said yes is:</p>", "options": ["0.16", "6.25", "1.6", "0.62"], "answer": 0, "expl": "40/250 = 0.16 (which is 16%). Counts → proportion → percent is the p̂ pipeline you'll use in every inference chapter."}, {"id": "D15", "skill": "notation", "stem": "<p>“x is at least 30” written in symbols is:</p>", "options": ["x &gt; 30", "x &lt; 30", "x ≤ 30", "x ≥ 30"], "answer": 3, "expl": "“At least” includes the boundary: x ≥ 30 (and yes, “at least 2” includes 2). Hypothesis-test setups live and die on this wording."}];
